hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"14521","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh: A weaver disputed to him, and a man who had given him yarn, but he spoiled its weaving. The weaver said: I have done well. He said: You get wage for what you did well, and he gets yarn like his yarn."
"14522","Ma'mar reported from Al-Zuhri and Ibn Shubruma: That they did not hold the shepherd liable."
"14523","From Al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Abu Awf, from Shurayh: While two men were spreading a garment, a man pushed a man onto the garment and tore it. Shurayh said: You are but like a stone, and he put the liability on the pusher."
"14524","Hushaym reported: Sayyar ibn Salama Abu al-Hakam informed me, from Al-Sha'bi: Two men disputed to Shurayh, one of whom had torn the garment of the other. Shurayh said: A patch for a patch."
"14525","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported from Makhul: That he used to hold the hired worker liable, even the innkeeper, who keeps people's animals for hire."
"14526","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur: I asked Ibrahim about a cobbler to whom I gave a sandal to sole without a wage, and the blade cut quickly into it. He saw no liability on him, because he took no wage for it; but if I had given him a wage, he would be liable."
"14527","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: A case was disputed to Shurayh about a sheep which a man gave to a man to hold for him until he comes to him. The man said: It escaped from me. Shurayh said: Your proof that it outran you while you were pursuing it. And he suspected him."
"14528","Ibn Uyayna reported from Mutarrif, from Al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh: He said: If [lacuna in source] the camel with its load, its owner is liable."
"14529","From Al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: They used to hold the hired worker liable, to the point that a man would buy a thing and say: Send my boy along with you, and he would say: No; so he would give him the wage so that he would be liable."
"14530","Al-Thawri said, and Ali ibn al-Aqmar informed me: He said: I disputed to Shurayh about a garment I gave to a dyer, and his house burned. He held him liable. He said: My house burned. Shurayh said: What if his house had burned, would you have left him your wage. He said: No. He said: Then pay him for his garments."
"14531","Al-Thawri said, and al-A'mash informed me, from Ibrahim: Some of them used to take goods as a consignment, and a wage was given over it so that he would guarantee it."
"14532","He said: Isra'il reported from Jabir: Amir was asked about the owner of a camel who carried people, and they drowned. He said: There is nothing on him."
"14533","He said: I heard Al-Thawri saying to Ma'mar: What did Ibn Sirin say about a man who hires from a man, then assigns it to another and profits over him. Ma'mar said: And Ayyub informed me that he heard Ibn Sirin, asked about that, and he said: All our brethren of the Kufans dislike it."
"14534","He said: Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ibn al-Musayyib, Salim ibn Abd Allah, and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr: Two of them disliked it, and two permitted it. I said: Who. He said: I do not know."
"14535","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he was asked about that, and he said: There is no harm in it."
"14536","Al-Thawri reported, and he asked him about a man who hires that, then leases it for more than that. Ubayda informed me, from Ibrahim, and Husayn, from Al-Sha'bi, and a man, from Mujahid: That they disliked it, unless he adds some work to it."
"14537","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Al-Hasan: There is no harm in it."
"14538","Ibrahim ibn Umar reported from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, from Ibrahim, Ibn Sirin, Shurayh, Al-Sha'bi, and Hammad: That they disliked a man hiring a boy, then leasing him for more than he hired him for."
"14539","Abd Allah reported from Shu'ba, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: It is riba."
"14540","Waki' reported from Al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: That he disliked it, and said: It belongs to its owner."
"14541","Isra'il reported from Jabir, from Abu Ja'far, about the tailor who takes the garment for a half or a third, then gives it for less. He said: If [lacuna in source] with something, then there is no harm in it."
"14542","Ma'mar reported from Qatada and Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: When a man hires out people a group, then hires for them another for less than he hired, and goes out with them, settling with them and departing, there is no harm in it if he does some work for them; but if he does not, then no."
"14543","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: A case was disputed to Shurayh about a man who bargained over a bow on condition that he draws it; he drew with it and it broke. Shurayh said: Whoever breaks a stick, it is his and one like it is on him. He said: Its owner had permitted. Shurayh said: Unless he permits."
"14544","Ibn Uyayna reported from Zakariyya, from Al-Sha'bi: Umar bargained with a man over a horse, and Umar put a rider of his on it to look at it, and the horse was injured. Umar said: It is yours. The other said: Rather it is yours. He said: Then appoint between me and you whom you wish. He said: Appoint between me and you Shurayh the Iraqi. They came to him, and Umar said: This one is pleased with you, and told him the story. Shurayh said: Take what you bought, or return as you took. Umar said: Is judgment anything but that. So Umar sent him as judge, and he was the first he sent."
"14545","From Al-Thawri, about a man who advances a man a dinar or dirhams for food, and finds the dirhams counterfeit. He said: The sale is void. And if you advance a man ten dirhams for two farqs, wheat and barley, and find five counterfeit, the sale is void, because you do not know whether the barley is it or the wheat. But if he separates them, five for wheat and five for barley, and finds counterfeit in them, he returns that in which he found the counterfeit."
"14546","From Al-Thawri, about a man who advances a man two dinars for a suit of known measure, and brings one of the two dinars counterfeit. He said: He voids the sale; but if it were food, it would be proper to take part of it and leave part. And when you advance dirhams for a thing to a term, and there is counterfeit among your dirhams, it is returned to you and there drops from the sale in proportion to what was returned to you by that reckoning, and what remains of the good dirhams stays on account of what you advanced for."
"14547","From Al-Thawri: When he says: Sell me this garment of yours for these hundred dirhams, and when he pays the dirhams they are counterfeit. He said: The sale binds him and he owes him good dirhams. Al-Thawri said: When a man says to a man: Sell me your goods for these dirhams, and shows them to him while they are fine-looking but deficient, there is no harm if you have shown them to him."
"14548","From Al-Thawri, from Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Silver for silver, weight for weight, and gold for gold, weight for weight. And any man whose silver is counterfeited should not go out swearing to people over it that it is good; but let him say: Who sells me a worn-out garment for these counterfeits."
"14549","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: Umar forbade silver except like for like. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf or al-Zubayr said to him: Coins are counterfeited on us; we give the bad and take the good. He said: Do not do so; but go to al-Baqi', sell your silver for a garment or goods, and when you take possession, then sell it, deduct what you wish and take what you wish."
"14550","From Al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: When a counterfeit dirham fell into the hand of Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, he broke it and said: No Muslim shall be deceived by you."
"14551","He said: Abu Ja'far al-Razi reported from Rabi' ibn Anas: I saw Safwan ibn Muhriz come to the market with a counterfeit dirham, and he said: Who sells me good merchandise for a bad dirham. So he bought, and did not call to witness. And Al-Thawri mentioned, from Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin: There is no harm in it when he discloses it."
"14552","Ma'mar reported from Al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade two sales and two garments. As for the two garments: wrapping in one garment, he wraps in a single garment, placing both ends of the garment on his left shoulder and baring his right side, and the other is to sit with knees drawn up in a single garment with nothing else on him, exposing his private part to the sky. As for the two sales: munabadha and mulamasa. And munabadha is that he says: When I cast this garment, the sale is binding; and mulamasa is that he holds it with his hand without spreading or turning it; when he touches it, the sale is binding. I said to Abu Bakr: meaning he bares his right side like idtiba'. He said: Yes, except that idtiba' is by gathering the garment under his armpit."
"14553","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade two garments and two sales. As for the two garments: wrapping of samma', and sitting with knees drawn up in a single garment, exposing his private part to the sky. As for the two sales: munabadha and mulamasa."
"14554","From Al-Thawri, from Ibn Dhakwan, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayra: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade two sales: limas and nibadh. And limas is touching the garment, and nibadh is casting the garment."
"14555","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, thus he said, and the correct is Umar ibn Sa'd, that he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mulamasa and munabadha. And mulamasa is touching the garment without looking at it; and munabadha is that the man casts the garment to the man in sale before turning and looking at it."
"14556","Ibn Jurayj reported: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Ata ibn Mina narrating, from Abu Hurayra, that he said: Fasting two days and two garments were forbidden. As for the two days: the day of Fitr and the day of Sacrifice. As for the two sales: mulamasa and munabadha. As for mulamasa: each of them touches the garment of his fellow without spreading; and munabadha: each of them casts his garment to the other, neither looking at the garment of his fellow. As for the two garments: that a man sits with knees drawn up in a single garment, exposing himself. Amr said: They hold that if he covers his private part there is no harm. As for the other garment: that he casts the inside of his waist-wrap and its outside over one of his shoulders, baring the side of his flank."
"14557","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Amr: Even if he gathers both ends of his garment on his right side. He said: I have seen them but disliking that."
"14558","From Al-Thawri, about a man who buys a hundred garments for a thousand dirhams, then returns one garment. He said: He may not sell them on murabaha."
"14559","From Al-Thawri, about goods between two men: half of it stands upon one of them at a hundred, and half upon the other at fifty, and they sell it on murabaha. The owner of the hundred gets two-thirds of the profit, and the owner of the fifty a third of the profit. Likewise if they sell with ten-twelve profit; and if they sell it by bargaining, the capital and the profit are between them halves and halves."
"14560","From Al-Thawri: Al-Hakam and Al-Sha'bi were asked about goods between two men, standing upon one of them at what it stands upon the other, and they sell it on murabaha. Al-Hakam said: The profit is halves. And Al-Sha'bi said: The profit is upon the capital. Al-Sha'bi said: And if they sell by bargaining, the capital and the profit are between them halves. And the saying of Al-Sha'bi is dearer to Al-Thawri."
"14561","From Al-Thawri: When you buy a garment for a hundred, then err and say: I bought for a hundred and fifty, and your profit is fifty, then it is discovered. He drops the fifty and its profit, and he gets the hundred and its profit; that is, two-thirds of the profit."
"14562","Al-Thawri said, about a man asked: For how much did you buy this slave. He said: For a hundred. A man said: You get profit of ten. Then proof comes to him that he took it for fifty. He said: If he does not deny, he takes the fifty and half the profit; and if he denies, the sale is returned against him."
"14563","From Al-Thawri, about a man who buys goods on credit, then sells it on murabaha, then it is discovered. He said: I heard from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Shurayh: He gets like his cash and like his term. He said: And our companions said: He has the choice, if he wishes he takes, and if he wishes he leaves; but if he consumes the goods, it is for cash."
"14564","From Hisham, from Muhammad, from Shurayh: He gets like his cash and like his term."
"14565","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: When you take goods on credit, or your fellow gives you respite, and you sell it on murabaha, inform your buyer of what you know. Ma'mar said, and Qatada said: If you conceal it then it is discovered, he gets the like of what you sold him of the credit."
"14566","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He disliked one saying: I sell you with profit of so-and-so and the exchange; for black dirhams and white ones, between them is a great difference, so he says: he exchanged the white."
"14567","From Al-Thawri, about one who buys goods with Kufan dinars, then comes to Syria and is asked: For how much did you take it. He says: For so-and-so. It is said: You get profit of five. He said: He gets the capital with which he bought, Kufan, and he gets the profit, Syrian."
"14568","From Al-Thawri: Every sale bought by a group of people, they may not sell part of it on murabaha. And when two buy goods then divide them by valuation, each taking his share, he may not sell it on murabaha, because another had bought with him."
"14569","Ma'mar reported: I was informed that Ibn Mas'ud disliked taking profit for maintenance."
"14570","Ma'mar reported from Qatada: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about the sale of ten for twelve. He said: There is no harm in it, so long as he does not take for maintenance."
"14571","He said: Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from Nuh ibn Abi Bilal: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: There is no harm in the ten-twelve sale, so long as he does not count the hire."
"14572","From Al-Thawri, from al-Qa'qa', from Ibrahim: We used to dislike it, then we saw no harm in it."
"14573","Isma'il ibn Abd Allah reported: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ajlan informed me, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, that he said: There is no harm in taking profit for maintenance."
"14574","Sufyan said: The profit of maintenance is the washer's wage and the like."
"14575","Al-Thawri reported from Ammar al-Duhni, from Ibn Abi Nu'm, from Ibn Umar: The ten-twelve sale is riba."
"14576","Ibn Uyayna reported from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid: I heard Ibn Abbas disliking the ten-eleven sale. He said: And that is the sale of the non-Arabs."
"14577","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, Ha, and from Al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Ibn Sirin: There is no harm in the ten-twelve sale, and maintenance is counted upon the garments."
"14578","From Al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, and from Ja'da ibn Dhakwan, from Shurayh: There is no harm in ten-twelve. Sufyan said: And the saying of Shurayh and Ibrahim, with the valuation, is dearer to me."
"14579","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: That he disliked one saying: Profit me upon the mark; and there is no harm in saying: Increase me upon the mark so-and-so."
"14580","From Al-Thawri, from Salim al-Dabbi, from Ibrahim: There is no harm in marking upon the garment more than it stood him at, and selling it on murabaha; there is no harm in sale upon the mark."
"14581","Isma'il ibn Abd Allah reported: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ajlan informed me: I asked Ibrahim al-Nakha'i: A man buys cloth by its mark, and adds to its mark his hire and other, then sells it on murabaha upon the mark. He said: Does he not look at the goods and spread them. I said: Yes. He said: There is no harm in it."
"14582","Al-Thawri reported: Wasil ibn Sulaym informed me, from Tawus, that he disliked it, and said: I will not sell my goods with lying."
"14583","Ma'mar reported from Al-Zuhri, Qatada, Ayyub, and Ibn Sirin: That they saw no harm in the valuation sale, that he says: Sell this for so-and-so; what exceeds is yours."
"14584","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from Al-Sha'bi, about a man saying: Sell this garment for so-and-so; what exceeds is yours. He said: There is no harm in it. Hushaym reported: I heard Amr ibn Dinar narrating, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that he saw no harm in it. And Yunus mentioned it, from Al-Hasan; and the valuation sale is that he says: Sell this for so-and-so; what exceeds is yours."
"14585","From Al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he disliked one saying: Sell this for so-and-so; what exceeds is yours."
"14586","Ma'mar and Al-Thawri reported from Hammad: He disliked it. He said: He hires him for a day, or assigns him something."
"14587","Ma'mar and Al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Abu Hurayra, or one of them, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever hires a hired worker, his hire is not his."
"14588","I said to Al-Thawri: Did you hear Hammad narrating, from Ibrahim, from Abu Sa'id, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever hires a hired worker, let him name his hire to him. He said: Yes; and he narrated it another time, but did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"14589","Ibn al-Taymi reported from his father, from Ibn Sirin: There is no harm in a man saying: Collect for me; whatever you collect of a thing, a third of it is yours, or a fourth."
"14590","Ma'mar reported from Al-Zuhri: When the bidder goes off with the garment, he may not take it for himself until he returns to its owner and tells him that."
"14591","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from Al-Sha'bi, about a man who says to a man: Sell this garment for so-and-so, and he sells it for less. He said: The sale is valid, and he guarantees what fell short."
"14592","Ibn Uyayna reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: When you price in cash and sell in cash, there is no harm in it; and when you price in cash and sell on credit, then no; that is but silver for silver. Ibn Uyayna said: I narrated it to Ibn Shubruma, and he said: I see no harm in it. Amr said: Ibn Abbas means: He may not price in cash, then sell for himself on debt."
"14593","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He disliked inheritance being sold among outbidders for other than the heirs, and saw no harm in it for the heirs."
"14594","Al-Thawri reported from Yunus, from Ibn Sirin, and from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: About the sale of outbidding, there is no harm in it, in inheritance and other."
"14595","Ibn Uyayna reported from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: There is no harm in the sale of outbidding; thus the fifths used to be sold."
"14596","Ma'mar reported: Ja'far ibn Burqan informed us: I heard Maymun ibn Mihran saying: There is no harm in the sale of outbidding; it is but his choice."
"14597","Ma'mar reported from Al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The pledge shall not be forfeited from him who pledges it. I said to Al-Zuhri: What of his saying, The pledge shall not be forfeited: is it the man saying, If I do not bring you your wealth, this pledge is yours. He said: Yes. Ma'mar said: Then it reached me from him that he said: If it perishes, the right of this one does not go; it perished from the owner of the pledge; its gain is his, and its loss is on him."
"14598","From Al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The pledge shall not be forfeited from him who pledges it; its gain is his, and its loss is on him."
"14599","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh: A man pledged his house for five hundred dirhams, and the owner of the dirhams said: If you do not bring me my wealth by such-and-such, your house is mine for what I claim of you. He did not come that day, and came after that. They disputed to Shurayh, and Shurayh said: If his hand misses his foot, his house is gone. Return his house to him, and take your wealth."
"14600","Ibn Uyayna reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus: He was asked about the pledge, a man pledging a thing, saying: If I do not bring it to you by such-and-such day, the pledge is likewise. He said: The pledge is not that; the pledge is sold, his right is given, and the excess is returned."
"14601","Ma'mar reported from Jabir, from Al-Sha'bi: A man pledged an iron ring for a brass pot, and it perished. They disputed to Shurayh, and he said: The pledge is for what is in it. Al-Sha'bi said: That is a thousand for a dirham, and a dirham for a thousand. Ma'mar said: And Al-Hasan used to say: The pledge is gone for what is in it."
"14602","From Al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, and Shurayh: The pledge is gone for what is in it. Al-Sha'bi said: And that is a dirham for a thousand, and a thousand for a dirham."
"14603","From Al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Ali: They return the excess between them. Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from Ali, the like; his saying, They return the excess, means: When he lends him a debt upon a pledge, worth ten for a dinar, and it is gone, its price is between them in halves."
"14604","From Al-Thawri, from al-Qa'qa', from Ibrahim: If the pledge is more, it is gone for what is in it; and if it is less, he returns the excess to him. Al-Thawri said: And we are upon that. Ma'mar reported from Qatada, and Ibrahim, the like."
"14605","Ma'mar reported from Al-Hasan, Al-Zuhri, Qatada, and Ibn Tawus, from his father: Whoever takes an animal as pledge, and it perishes, it is for what is in it."
"14606","From Al-Thawri, about a man who takes a slave as pledge, and he runs away. He said: He is liable. And Layth said, from Tawus: And if he dies, he is liable."
"14607","Ma'mar reported from Al-Zuhri and Qatada: When an animal is pledged, it is like any other."
"14608","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Shubruma, from Al-Sha'bi, about an animal pledged which dies. He said: Nothing of his right goes; he returns upon its owner."
"14609","From Al-Thawri: When he pledges you an animal for ten dinars, and he gives you the dinars, then you stand to bring them. He said: It is in the liability of the pledge-holder until he returns it and takes back the dinars from him."
"14610","Ma'mar reported from Jabir, from Amir, and from a man, from Al-Hasan: When he places it in the hand of another and it perishes, it is for what is in it. He said: And they were asked, is he more entitled to it, or the creditors. They said: He is more entitled to it."
"14611","From Al-Thawri, from Ash'ath: Al-Hakam and Al-Sha'bi differed about a pledge placed in the hands of a trustworthy third party. Al-Hakam said: It is not a pledge. And Al-Sha'bi said: It is a pledge. And Ibn Abi Layla takes the saying of Al-Hakam. Ma'mar reported from Qatada the like."
"14612","Ma'mar reported from Qatada: If it perishes in the hand of another, it is not possessed. He said: He and the creditors are equal in it."
"14613","Ma'mar reported from Qatada: Whoever takes a thing as pledge and takes possession of it, he is more entitled to it than the creditors."
"14614","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from Al-Sha'bi: When the pledge-holder takes possession, then the pledgor dies owing debt, he is more entitled to it than the creditors."
"14615","Ma'mar reported: Qatada was asked about a man who pledges two anklets, and one of them perishes. He said: His right is in the remaining of them."
"14616","Al-Thawri reported from al-Qa'qa', from Ibrahim: He said about the pledge: When it is more, then something of it goes, there goes from the right in proportion to what went from the pledge; and when the right is more, there goes from the right what went from the pledge."
"14617","Al-Thawri reported, about a man who pledges a man a pledge, and the pledgor gives part of the right, then the pledge perishes. He said: He returns what he took of the right. He said: And with it we take."
"14618","Ma'mar reported: And Qatada was asked about a man who takes a slave-girl as pledge. He said: He may not touch her. I said to him: She flees from him who took her as pledge to her master, and he has intercourse with her and she conceives. He said: She is sold if her master has no wealth. He said: And he ransoms his child. Ma'mar said: And I asked Ibn Shubruma about her, and he said: She is made to earn, and not sold."
"14619","From Al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from Al-Sha'bi, about a man who pledges a slave-girl, then goes to her. He said: He disliked that. Sufyan said: And we say: If she conceives from her master, he has consumed her."
"14620","From Al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Al-Sha'bi: When she gives birth, the child is of the pledge; it is but an increase in her."
"14621","Ma'mar reported from Al-Hasan: When the pledgor and the pledge-holder differ, the word is the word of the pledgor."
"14622","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Hashim, from a man, from Ibrahim: When the pledge-holder and the pledgor differ, and the pledgor says: I pledged it to you for a dirham, and the pledge-holder says: I took it as pledge for a thousand, the word is the word of the pledgor, because the pledge-holder claims the excess. But if the pledge perishes, the word is the word of the pledge-holder, unless the pledgor brings proof of the value of his pledge. Sufyan said: And our companions say it."
"14623","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Shubruma: The word is the word of the pledgor."
"14624","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Al-Zuhri, and from Qatada: They said: When the pledgor and the pledge-holder [predicate lacuna in source] which is in his hands, unless it reaches the value of the pledge, unless the other brings proof."
"14625","Ma'mar reported from Al-Zuhri and Ibn Shubruma, about a man who pledges a thing, then says: It is a deposit, while the other says: Rather it is a pledge. He said: It is a deposit, unless the other brings proof that it is a pledge."
"14626","Al-Thawri reported, about a man who takes a garment as pledge, and takes dirhams from him, then says to the owner of the garment: Lend me to wear it, and it perishes. He said: When he returns it and the pledge is gone, it is from the wealth of the pledgor."
"14627","Ma'mar reported from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra: The pledge is ridden, milked, and fed. Al-A'mash said: I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he disliked benefiting from the pledge at all."
"14628","From Al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Safar, from Al-Sha'bi, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the pledge: The milk and the back are ridden and milked for its fodder."
"14629","From Al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, and Isma'il, from Al-Sha'bi: That they both disliked benefiting from the pledge at all."
"14630","From Al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh: He was asked, what is drinking riba. He said: A man pledges a cow, then drinks its milk."
"14631","Ibn Uyayna reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim: They did not take from the hadith of Abu Hurayra except so-and-so, and the pledge is ridden and milked."
"14632","Al-Thawri reported from Khalid, from Ibn Sirin: A man came to Ibn Mas'ud and said: A man pledged me a mare and I rode it. He said: Whatever you got of its back is riba."
"14633","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father: He said, in the letter of Mu'adh ibn Jabal: Whoever takes land as pledge, its fruit is counted for the owner of the pledge, from the year of the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"14634","Al-Thawri reported from Zakariyya: Al-Sha'bi was asked about a man who takes a slave-girl as pledge, and she suckles for him. He said: He pays the owner of the slave-girl the value of the suckling of the milk."
"14635","Ma'mar reported from Qatada: He disliked pledging the mushaf; but if he does, there is no harm in reading in it."
"14636","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: The pledge is not sold except before the authority."
"14637","From Al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha: Muhammad ibn Sirin said to me: I have pledged yarn with me; go to Iyas ibn Mu'awiya, he was judge then, and ask his permission for me to sell it, for I fear spoilage for it; so he permitted him."
"14638","From Al-Thawri: The judge looks for the absent in the pledge feared to spoil."
"14639","Sufyan said: If its owner permits in the pledge, he sells it; otherwise it is sold before the authority; and when the trustworthy third party sells the pledge, it is valid."
"14640","Ma'mar reported from Jabir, from Amir, about a man who pledges a pledge and places it in the hands of a trustworthy third party. He said: That is to him; if he wishes he sells it justly, and if he wishes he does not sell it."
"14641","Ma'mar reported from Jabir, from Amir, from Shurayh, that he used to say to the owner of the pledge: You know best; if you see fit to sell, sell."
"14642","From Al-Thawri: When he pledges you two garments for ten, and he brings five and says: Give me half the pledge. He said: Do not give him until you fulfill your right, because the origin was for the whole right."
"14643","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, about a man who gives a man wealth as qirad, and travel is established between him and him, and he goes out: Upon whom is the maintenance. He said: The maintenance is in the wealth, the profit upon what they agree, and the loss upon the wealth."
"14644","Al-Thawri reported from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin: Whatever the agent eats is a debt upon him."
"14645","Al-Thawri said, and Ash'ath informed me, from Ibrahim: He eats and clothes reasonably. And al-Rabi' said, from Al-Hasan: He eats reasonably."
"14646","Al-Thawri reported, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: Whatever allowance the muqarad (mudaraba agent) makes in a transaction is charged against the capital."
"14647","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Sirin and Abu Qilaba, who both said about mudaraba (profit-sharing): the loss is upon the capital, and the profit is according to what they agreed upon. Ma'mar reported, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, and from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, the same."
"14648","He said: al-Qays ibn al-Rabi' said, from Abu al-Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Ali, about mudaraba: the loss is upon the capital, and the profit is according to what they agreed upon. As for al-Thawri, he mentioned it from Abu Husayn, from 'Ali, about mudaraba or two partners."
"14649","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, who said: There is no profit for the muqarad until he settles accounts with the owner of the capital, and whatever loss there is, it is upon the capital."
"14650","Al-Thawri reported, from Abu Husayn, and from Hashim Abu Kulayb, and from Ibrahim, and Isma'il al-Asadi, from al-Sha'bi, and 'Asim al-Ahwal, from Jabir ibn Zayd, who said: The profit is according to what they agreed upon, and the loss is upon the capital; this is regarding two partners, for example one contributes one hundred and the other two hundred."
"14651","Al-Thawri said, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, about two men each of whom put forward ten thousand and entered partnership but did not mix their funds, then one of them worked with what he had and it perished: he did not consider it a partnership. He said: The loss and destruction are upon him, and nothing is upon the other. Sufyan said: That is because they did not mix their funds."
"14652","'Abdullah reported, from Shu'ba, from a man, who said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: When a man takes another as partner in a sale, then if there is profit it is his, and if there is a loss nothing is upon him; it is only a favour granted to him."
"14653","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about two men each of whom put forward one hundred dinars and entered partnership, then one of them worked with it. He said: The one who worked gets the profit of one hundred, and half the profit of the other hundred. Ma'mar said: Others said: The profit is between them, and this is more beloved to us."
"14654","Ma'mar reported, from Hammad, who said: If he did not stipulate zakat upon him, then zakat is upon the owner of the capital."
"14655","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, who said: The maintenance of the muqarad is upon the capital."
"14656","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he disliked cloth goods as mudaraba capital, saying: No, except gold and silver. Sufyan said: And we say: He gets a fair wage if he is given merchandise as mudaraba."
"14657","Ma'mar reported, from Hammad, about a man who gives cloth goods as mudaraba. He said: The basis of their qirad is upon what he sells the merchandise for."
"14658","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He used to dislike giving merchandise as a loan with a fixed term, fearing he might sell it for less than that and then say: I sold for what you ordered me. He said: And al-Hasan said: You were entrusted with something and you entered into it."
"14659","From al-Thawri, from Hisham and Ibn 'Awn, that Ibn Sirin permitted working with cloth goods as mudaraba for a single transaction; when he has traded with it, the profit is between them, and he returns his principal, then if he wishes he may hand it to him again afterwards."
"14660","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin and Abu Qilaba, who said about a man who gave a man wealth as mudaraba, then part of it was lost or diminished in value. They said: If the owner of the capital had not settled accounts with him before he traded again with it and gained profit, then there is no profit for the muqarad until the owner of the capital recovers his principal in full; but if he had already settled with him or hired him and then he traded again, they divide the profit between them, and the earlier loss is upon the capital. He said: Ibn al-Taymi said, from 'Awf, from Ibn Sirin, the same."
"14661","'Awf said, and al-Hasan said: If any of it perishes, whether he informed the owner of the capital or not, then he trades with it a second time, they divide the profit earned in the second venture, and the loss falls specifically upon the original principal."
"14662","From al-Thawri, about a man who gave a man one thousand dirhams, then he brought one thousand dirhams and said: This is profit, and I already paid you your one thousand as principal, while he has no proof, and the owner of the capital says: You have not yet paid me my principal. He said: He has no profit until this one recovers the principal, unless he brings proof that he already paid him his principal."
"14663","Ma'mar reported, from a man who gave a man wealth as mudaraba, then he brought him the capital along with his share of profit and handed it to him, then claimed he made a mistake. He said: Once the wealth has left his hands, he is not believed."
"14664","From al-Thawri, about a man who gave another wealth as mudaraba, and the owner of the capital says: On a third, while the other says: On a half. He said: The word is the word of the owner of the capital, unless the other brings proof."
"14665","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh: When a man came to him saying: This man betrayed me, he would say: Your proof that your trustee betrayed you. This is about mudaraba. And when he said to him: Such-and-such calamity befell me, he would say: Your proof of a calamity after its Lord."
"14666","From al-Thawri, about a man who gave a man wealth as mudaraba without stipulating anything, and he worked with the wealth. He said: He gets a fair wage."
"14667","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: When the mudarib violates the terms, he becomes liable."
"14668","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said about the mudarib: When he transgresses, the liability is upon the one who transgressed, and the profit is as they stipulated. And this is the view of Ma'mar."
"14669","From al-Thawri, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Awn, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, who said: It is his by virtue of his liability, and his secret dealings are his own, so he is believed in it."
"14670","Al-Thawri said, and 'Asim said, from al-Sha'bi: It is his by virtue of his liability."
"14671","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: Liability is upon the one who transgressed, and the profit belongs to the owner of the capital."
"14672","Ma'mar said, and I heard Hammad saying: The profit is lawful for neither of them, and liability is upon the one who transgressed. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Shubruma said it as well."
"14673","From Ibn 'Uyayna, from 'Amr, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, who said about the mudarib: When he violates the terms, he becomes liable."
"14674","From Ibn al-Taymi, from one who heard Qatada narrating, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from 'Ali, who said: Whoever shares the profit has no liability upon him."
"14675","Ma'mar reported, from al-Hasan and Qatada, about the mudarib whom the owner of the capital forbade to buy livestock and to descend into a valley bottom. He said: He is a man who intended good. He said: He is not liable."
"14676","Abu Sufyan Waki' reported, from Hammad ibn Salama, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayra, who said: If the owner of the capital stipulated upon him not to descend into a valley bottom, and he descended and perished, then he is liable."
"14677","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, about the mudarib when he transgresses. He said: I did not see them holding him liable when he was acting in the interest of the owner of the capital."
"14678","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, about a man who entered qirad on half, then the other went and entered qirad on a quarter. He said: What he entered as qirad is his share, unless he had held back part of the wealth in his hand and gave that as a stake for himself and his partner."
"14679","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, about a man who took wealth from a man as mudaraba, worked with it, and mixed his own wealth into it without the other knowing. He said: If the wealth perishes, there is no liability upon him, and if there is profit in it, it is by shares. From Ibn al-Taymi, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, the same."
"14680","From al-Thawri, about a man who entered qirad with a man on half, then that one went and entered qirad with another on a quarter. He said: He may not hand it over except with his permission, otherwise he is liable, unless he said to him: Deal with it as Allah shows you, for then he has permitted him."
"14681","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from Layth, from Tawus, and Humayd, from al-Hasan, who said: The mudarib is a trustee, even if he goes against your order."
"14682","Ibrahim ibn 'Umar reported, from 'Abd al-Karim, from Ibrahim, who said about the mudarib: When he goes beyond what he was ordered, he is liable. He said: And Ibrahim said: The profit is lawful for neither of them. 'Abd al-Karim said: And al-Hasan said: If he intended thereby benefit, there is no liability."
"14683","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Mazini reported, from al-Thawri, who said: If a man gives a man one thousand dirhams as mudaraba, and he buys a slave-girl with it, and she pleases him, and he has intercourse with her and she bears him a child, she is valued; if there is surplus in her over one thousand dirhams, we hold him liable for the value of the slave-girl and lift from him his share in the slave-girl, since he has a portion in her, and the child is his; but if there is no surplus in her, upon him is the dower, the hadd is warded off from him due to doubt, and the child is owned by the owner of the capital, since he had intercourse with her while he had no share in her."
"14684","Ma'mar reported, who said: One who heard Maymun ibn Mihran informed me, saying to the muqarads: Do not buy on credit, for if you buy, you are liable for what you bought on credit."
"14685","From al-Thawri, about a man who gave a man wealth as muqarada and said: Buy on credit on my account. He said: That is disliked, since he guaranteed for him while it brings him benefit."
"14686","Ma'mar reported, from a man who gave a man wealth as mudaraba and permitted him to buy on credit jointly between them, then he bought for one hundred dinars, and the muqarada venture perished along with what was bought on credit. He said: As for what was bought on credit and perished, it is between them, and the wealth given to him as muqarada that perished is upon the owner of the capital."
"14687","Ma'mar reported, who said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who entered qirad with a man, bought goods, placed them in the house, then said to the owner of the capital: Come to me tomorrow, then a thief came and stole the goods and the wealth. He said: I do not think the owners of the wealth are charged more than their wealth; the loss is upon the buyer."
"14688","From al-Thawri, about a man who entered qirad with a man, bought goods, placed them in the house, then said to the owner of the capital: Come to me tomorrow, then the thief came and stole the goods. He said: The owner of the capital takes the muqarad, and the muqarad takes the owner of the capital."
"14689","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: There is no harm in giving a man wealth as muqarada while he carries goods for you as an errand."
"14690","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he disliked it."
"14691","Al-Thawri reported, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he disliked giving one thousand as mudaraba, one thousand as a loan, and one thousand as goods for sale on commission; but if there was no stipulation, there is no harm in it."
"14692","From al-Thawri, about a man given wealth as mudaraba on a third, or a quarter, or whatever they mutually agree. He said: It is his wealth; he may stipulate in it whatever he wishes."
"14693","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He used to dislike the one to whom wealth was given buying on credit from the owner of the capital, but he did not dislike the owner of the capital buying on credit from the muqarad."
"14694","From al-Thawri, who said: When the price is established, the owner of the capital has the most right to it if there is profit in it. This is about the muqarad buying from his own qirad venture, and the stipulation is void, such as saying: Whatever you bring that pleases me, I take at the price."
"14695","Al-Thawri said, about a man who gave a man one thousand dirhams as mudaraba on half, then after a day gave him another thousand on half. He said: Each thousand stands alone."
"14696","Isma'il ibn 'Abdullah reported, who said: Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida informed me, who said: A dispute was brought to al-Sha'bi about a man who gave a man four thousand dirhams as mudaraba. The one to whom it was given set out with it, and the owner of the capital had him witness that he had nothing with him except his wealth. The man went on his journey, then returned, then death came to him, and he bequeathed that the wealth with him from the four thousand belonged to so-and-so, while people came who had given him wealth before that. Al-Sha'bi judged for the owner of the four thousand with the wealth that was with the mudarib, saying: He had him witness before he set out that he had nothing with him except his wealth, and the mudarib acknowledged it was his wealth."
"14697","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, about a man's partner in a commodity, whose partnership is only in that commodity, who sold the commodity without his partner's permission. He said: His partner's share is not binding except with his permission; if he permitted him to sell and then he rescinded the sale, he has no right to that; and if he had informed him of the sale, his rescission is not valid in his partner's share. But if it is a mufawada partnership, the dealing of each one is binding upon his partner in selling, buying, and rescission."
"14698","Al-Thawri reported, from Ash'ath, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Mufawada is in all wealth. And Ibn Sirin used to reject inheritance, saying: It belongs to whoever inherits it, when one of the two mufawada partners inherits. He said: And Ibn Abi Layla used to say: When one of the two mufawada partners inherits wealth, he shares it with the other."
"14699","Hushaym reported, from Sayyar Abu al-Hakam, from al-Sha'bi, who said: The sale of every partner is valid in his partnership, except the partner by inheritance."
"14700","Sufyan reported, who said: There is no mufawada until they are equal in wealth and until they mix their funds. And there is no mufawada or partnership with merchandise, where one brings goods and another brings goods, unless there is between them a slave, a house, gold, or silver, and they mix them and enter mufawada in it and in everything; this is mufawada. And if there were dinars or dirhams between them, there is no mufawada until they mix them. Whatever one of the two mufawada partners buys on credit and says: I bought such-and-such on credit, he is believed against his partner. If one of them dies, the other takes over, and if the creditor wishes he may take either of them. Whoever of them sells his commodity, the buyer may take whichever of them he wishes. And it is not mufawada for a man to say: Whatever I and you buy is between me and you, without mixing anything; in that case, whatever either of them claims he bought, he is asked for proof that he bought on behalf of his partner, and if he wishes he may abandon him."
"14701","From al-Thawri, who said: Every sale in which there is no measure, weight, or count, its harvesting, carrying, and loss are upon the buyer; and every sale in which there is measure, weight, or count remains upon the seller until he delivers it in full to him. So if a man says to a man: I sell you the fruit of this palm tree, its harvesting is upon the buyer."
"14702","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I witnessed Shurayh when two men came disputing about a sheep one sold the other for twenty dirhams while being a partner in it; then the buyer sold it for twenty-one dirhams, and the one who bought it went off with it and the dirham. They disputed to Shurayh, and he said to the one who sold: You intended usury but it did not yield usury for you; it was only a partnership in one dirham."
"14703","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: He used to dislike that you sell your merchandise, whatever it is, and then share in it for a quarter."
"14704","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, who used to say: There is no harm in saying for merchandise: I sell it while half of it is mine, or a quarter of it is mine."
"14705","Al-Taymi reported, from his father, who said: When he disliked saying: I sell you this while half of it is mine, but let him say: I sell you half of it. Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, the same."
"14706","Ma'mar said, about a man given wealth as mudaraba who sold it and made an exception of a share in it for himself, then disputed with him. He said: It is disliked to say: Your left hand sold to your right hand. And al-Hasan said: You were entrusted with something and entered into it."
"14707","Ma'mar reported, who said: I heard a shaykh called al-Zubayr Abu Salama saying: I heard Ibn 'Umar while selling his fruit, saying: I sell it to you for four thousand, along with the food of the young men who work it."
"14708","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, about a man to whom it is said: I sell you the fruit of my orchard for one hundred dinars except fifty faraqs. He disliked it, saying: Unless he stipulates known palm trees."
"14709","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ya'qub, from Ibrahim, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: It is disliked to sell palm trees while excepting a known measure from them. Sufyan said: But there is no harm in excepting this palm tree and that palm tree."
"14710","Malik reported, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that 'Amr ibn Hazm sold fruit for four thousand while stipulating some of its fruit for himself."
"14711","Abu Sufyan Waki' reported, from Isma'il ibn Mujammi', that he asked Salim ibn 'Abdullah about fruit he sold while excepting a measure from it. He said: There is no harm in it."
"14712","Isma'il ibn 'Abdullah reported, from Ibn 'Awn, that he asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: We did not see any harm in an exception, were it not that Ibn 'Umar disliked it, though he was approved among us; meaning selling the fruit of his palms while excepting known palm trees."
"14713","Ma'mar reported, who said: The people of Madina used to follow a rule in crop calamity, saying: Whatever is less than a third is upon the buyer up to a third; when it exceeds a third, it is a calamity. And I did not see them count as calamity except in fruits; for I mentioned to them cloth burning and slaves dying. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Zuhri informed me, who said: I said to him: What is a calamity? He said: Half."
"14714","Al-Aslami reported, from Husayn ibn 'Abdullah, from his father, from his grandfather, from 'Ali, who said: Calamity is a third or more, deducted from its owner, and whatever is less than that is upon him. And calamity means rain, wind, locusts, and fire."
"14715","From al-Thawri, about calamity for one who bought fruit after its ripeness appeared and took possession of it: it is in his guarantee."
"14716","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, who said: Whenever a man sells a man goods and the buyer goes bankrupt, if the seller finds his goods intact he has the most right to them; but if he had received anything of their price, he and the creditors are equal in them. And if the buyer dies, the seller is like the other creditors."
"14717","Malik reported, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whenever a man sells a man goods and the buyer goes bankrupt, while the seller has received nothing of the price, if the seller finds his goods intact he has the most right to them; but if the buyer dies, he is like the other creditors in them. Abu Sufyan reported, from Hisham, the companion of al-Dastuwai, who said: Qatada narrated to me, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as the hadith of al-Zuhri."
"14718","Malik reported, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whenever a man goes bankrupt and a man finds his wealth intact, he has the most right to it than others."
"14719","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abu Hurayra, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whenever a man goes bankrupt and with him are goods intact, their owner has the most right to them to the exclusion of the creditors."
"14720","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Hisham ibn Yahya, from Abu Hurayra, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man goes bankrupt and the seller finds his goods intact, he has the most right to them to the exclusion of the creditors. Muhammad ibn Muslim reported, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Hisham ibn Yahya, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Ibn 'Uyayna reported, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Hisham ibn Yahya, from Abu Hurayra, narrating the same."
"14721","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: When a man sells his goods to a man and the buyer goes bankrupt, he said: If he finds his goods intact in full, he has the most right to them; but if the buyer has consumed any of them, little or much, the seller is like the other creditors. And Ibn Jurayj said it from 'Ata."
"14722","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, about a man who consumes part of goods of which he bought a portion and goes bankrupt. He said: They belong to their owner to the exclusion of the creditors, whatever of them he finds, so long as he has not collected anything of his due."
"14723","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz said: If he had collected anything of their price, he and the creditors are equal in them. And al-Zuhri said it as well."
"14724","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: Whenever a creditor collects anything from him after his bankruptcy, he and the creditors are equal; he shares with them in it. And Ibn Sirin used to give fatwa accordingly."
"14725","Isra'il reported, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay', from Ibn Abi Mulayka, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sells goods to a man without receiving payment, then the man goes bankrupt, and he finds his goods intact, let him take them to the exclusion of the creditors."
"14726","Abu Sufyan, the companion of al-Dastuwai, reported, from Qatada, from Khilas, from 'Ali, who said: He is in them like the other creditors, when he finds them intact."
"14727","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: He and the creditors share alike in them. And al-Thawri holds this view, saying: Bankruptcy and death are with us the same. We take the view of Ibrahim."
"14728","Ma'mar reported, who said: We heard that the bankrupt, so long as he has not been publicly proclaimed, his dealings are valid; but once he is proclaimed, he has no disposal over his wealth."
"14729","From al-Thawri, from 'Amr ibn Maymun, that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz used to hire out the bankrupt for the humblest work, to reproach him thereby. Al-Thawri said: And Ibn Abi Layla used to detain him for people when informed he had hidden wealth but nothing appeared of it."
"14730","Ma'mar reported, from a man, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, who said: The sale and purchase of the interdicted person are valid, just as the hadd penalties are enforced upon him and he is held to hire."
"14731","From al-Thawri, who said: The sale and purchase of the bankrupt are valid, so long as the ruler has not declared him bankrupt; and if the interdicted person takes on debt, what he took on and did is valid. He says: No Muslim is interdicted."
"14732","A man informed me, who heard Hisham ibn 'Urwa narrating from his father, who said: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far came to al-Zubayr and said: I made a purchase for such-and-such, and 'Ali wants to go to 'Uthman and ask him to interdict me. Al-Zubayr said to him: Then I am your partner in the purchase. Then 'Ali came to 'Uthman and said to him: Ibn Ja'far made such-and-such purchase, so interdict him. Al-Zubayr said: I am his partner in this purchase. So 'Uthman said: How can I interdict a man in a purchase in which al-Zubayr is a partner?"
"14733","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, who said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal was a generous, young, handsome man, among the best youths of his people, and he kept nothing back and continued taking debts until debt consumed all his wealth. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him to ask his creditors to remit for him, but they refused. Had they remitted for anyone for anyone's sake, they would have remitted for Mu'adh ibn Jabal for the sake of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold all his wealth to settle his debt, until Mu'adh was left with nothing. Then in the year of the conquest of Makka, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him over a part of Yemen as a governor to compensate him. Mu'adh stayed in Yemen, and he was the first to trade with the wealth of Allah, and stayed until he prospered, until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away. When he passed away, 'Umar said to Abu Bakr: Send to this man, leave him what sustains him, and take the rest from him. Abu Bakr said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him only to compensate him, and I shall take nothing from him unless he gives me. So 'Umar went to Mu'adh when Abu Bakr did not obey him, and 'Umar mentioned that to Mu'adh. Mu'adh said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me only to compensate me, and I shall not do it. Then Mu'adh met 'Umar and said: I have obeyed you, and I shall do what you ordered me; I saw in a dream that I was in deep water fearing drowning, and you delivered me from it, O 'Umar. So Mu'adh came to Abu Bakr, mentioned that to him, and swore to him he had hidden nothing from him, even showing him his whip. Abu Bakr said: No, by Allah, I shall not take it from you; I have granted it to you. 'Umar said: This is when it became lawful and wholesome. He said: Then Mu'adh set out for Syria. Ma'mar said: A man of Quraysh informed me, who said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold the wealth of Mu'adh, he stopped people over it, saying: Whoever sells this anything, it is void."
"14734","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, or another, from al-Hasan, who said: There is no loss upon the due of a Muslim man; if he does not receive it, he returns to his fellow who transferred him."
"14735","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: It used to be said: There is no loss upon the wealth of a Muslim; he returns to his first debtor. This is about transfer of debt. He said: We said: Even if he took part of his due? He said: Even so, it used to be said: There is no loss upon the due of a Muslim."
"14736","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, about a man who transferred a man to another, but he paid him nothing. Shurayh said to the one who transferred: Your proof that you paid and it was paid on your behalf. He said: But he has released me. He said: Your proof that he released you from bankruptcy and wrongdoing that he knew."
"14737","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, that he disputed to Shurayh that a man transferred him to a man. He said: So I demanded from him, but he kept not paying me, so I disputed with him to Shurayh, and he sent me back to my first fellow."
"14738","Isra'il reported, from Abu Ishaq, who said: So-and-so owed me three hundred dirhams due from a man, but he delayed me six months, then gave me a pouch, saying: This is musk. I showed it to a neighbour of mine, who said: It is only ramik and sukk, worth only one hundred dirhams. He said: So I returned it to him, then came to my first seller. He said: So I went with him to Shurayh, and we sat before him. He said: He has released me. I said: I did release him, but he transferred me to a man who delayed me, then gave me a pouch of ramik which I returned to him. He said: Stand and give him his due."
"14739","I heard Ma'mar, or one who heard him informed me, narrating from Qatada, that 'Ali said: He returns to his fellow only if he goes bankrupt or dies."
"14740","Ibn Jurayj reported, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The defendant is most entitled to the oath when there is no proof."
"14741","Al-Thawri reported, from Ma'n ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that Ibn Mas'ud sold al-Ash'ath ibn Qays a sale, and they differed about the price. 'Abdullah said: For twenty, while al-Ash'ath said: For ten. 'Abdullah said: Appoint between me and you whomever you wish; appoint a man between me and you. Al-Ash'ath said: You are between me and yourself. 'Abdullah said: Then I say according to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged: When two sellers differ and there is no proof, the word is the word of the owner of the goods, and they annul the sale."
"14742","Ma'mar reported, who said: I asked Hammad about a man who bought a slave-girl and had intercourse with her, then the seller came saying: I sold you for one hundred dinars, while the other said: I bought her for fifty. He said: Now the proof is upon the seller."
"14743","Ma'mar reported, who said: I asked Hammad about a man who bought goods and they differed, while the goods had perished. He said: The proof of the seller, or the oath of the buyer; but if the goods are intact, both are made to swear and the sale is annulled."
"14744","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: When two sellers differ about the sale, both swear; if both swear, the sale is annulled; if one refuses and the other swears, it belongs to the one who swore; and if both refuse, the sale is annulled."
"14745","From al-Thawri, who said: One who heard Ibrahim informed me, saying: When two sellers differ and the goods have perished, the word is the word of the buyer, unless the seller brings proof; but if they are standing intact, and each establishes his proof, we take the proof of the one claiming the excess."
"14746","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, who said: It reached me from Shurayh that he said: The decisive word is: two witnesses upon the claimant, and the oath upon the one who denies."
"14747","Al-Thawri reported, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, who said: There is no proof upon the one from whom payment is sought."
"14748","Ibn 'Uyayna reported, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that the oath is upon the defendant."
"14749","Muhammad ibn Muslim reported, who said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, that two women were stitching leather in a house with no one else with them in the house. One of them came out with a stab in her palm from an awl that came out from the back of her palm, saying: Her companion stabbed her, while the other denied. I sent to Ibn 'Abbas and told him the news. He said: Give nothing except with proof, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had people been given by their claims, men would have claimed the wealth of men; rather the oath is upon the defendant. So call her and recite to her: Indeed those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price, the verse. So I did, and she confessed. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: Then I met Ibn Jurayj and he narrated it to me after a year."
"14750","Ma'mar reported, and he was asked about a man who bought two garments from a man, and he said to him: Go with them, and whichever you approve take at the price, then one of them perished. He said: I value this remaining one for him, and make the excess the price of the one that perished."
"14751","From al-Thawri, who said: When you buy two different garments from two men on approval, and each of them says: The better of the two garments is mine, then the word is the word of the one returning; he may return whichever of the two he wishes as the better. But if it is not known, the sale becomes binding, and he is made to swear which of them is the better."
"14752","From al-Thawri, about a man who sold two garments, then the buyer sold one garment and found a defect in the other, and established proof. The buyer said: The value of what was sold is such-and-such, while the other said: Rather such-and-such. The word is the word of the seller, unless the buyer brings proof. And Ma'mar said: If he wishes he may deduct the defect from him, otherwise he returns the remaining garment at a fair value."
"14753","I asked al-Thawri about a man who said to a man: I sold you my house when I was a boy, while the buyer said: Rather you sold me when you were a man. He said: The proof is upon the seller that he sold it when he was a boy; the sale is valid until the buyer voids it. The man said to him: But Malik said: The word is the word of the seller. But he paid no attention to him."
"14754","Al-Thawri said: When you buy a garment on approval and return it, and the owner of the garment says: This is not my garment, then the word is the word of the one returning."
"14755","He said: I asked Ma'mar about a man who paid a man a dinar, then he returned it to him saying: It is deficient, while the other said: I gave you a full-weight one. He said: If he gave it to him without proof, the word is the word of the one returning; but if he had witnesses to the clearance, the word is the word of the payer, unless the other brings proof that it is deficient."
"14756","Ma'mar said, about a man who said to a man: I lent you a dinar, while the other said: Rather you gifted it to me. He said: It is a loan unless the other brings proof that he gifted it to him. And Ma'mar said about a man who found goods with a man and said: They were stolen from me, while the other said: You pawned them with me. He said: The word is for the one who said: They were stolen from me."
"14757","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Tamim ibn Tarafa, that two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a camel, and each of them established two witnesses, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it between them."
"14758","Isra'il reported, who said: Simak ibn Harb informed us, that he heard Tamim ibn Tarafa al-Ta'i saying: Two men came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) both claiming a camel, and each established two witnesses that it was born to him and belonged to him, so he judged it between them."
"14759","From al-Thawri, from 'Alqama ibn Marthad, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: I was with Abu al-Darda when two men disputed to him about a horse, and each established proof that it was his horse born to him, and that he neither sold nor gifted it. Abu al-Darda said: One of you is surely lying. Then he divided it between them in halves. Abu al-Darda said: How much you need the chain like the chain of the Children of Isra'il, which used to descend and seize the neck of the wrongdoer."
"14760","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about two men who claimed an animal, each establishing proof that it was his animal. He said: It belongs to the one in whose hands it is, or he said: Whoever acknowledges something in his hands, the word is his."
"14761","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: Two men disputed to him about a horse both claimed, in the possession of one of them, and each established proof that it was born to him. Shurayh said: The breeder has more right than the recognizer. And he gave it to the one in whose hands it was, saying: These kept seeing it in his hands, while those only recognized it by their claim."
"14762","Isra'il reported, from Simak ibn Harb, from Hanash ibn al-Mu'tamir, from 'Ali, who said: Two men came to him disputing about a mule. One brought five witnesses that it was born to him, while the other brought two witnesses that it was born to him. He said to the people with him: What do you think, shall I judge by the more numerous witnesses, though the two witnesses may be better than the five? Then he said: In it there is a judgment and a settlement, and I shall tell you of the judgment and the settlement. As for the settlement: it is divided between them, five shares for this one and two shares for that one. As for the judgment: one of them swears along with his witnesses and takes the mule, and if he wishes he may swear emphatically and then take the mule."
"14763","From al-Hasan ibn 'Umara, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, who said: Two men disputed to 'Ali about an animal in the possession of one of them; each established proof that it was his animal. He judged it for the one in whose hands it was. He said: And 'Ali said: If it is not in the hands of either of them, and each establishes that it is his animal, then it is between them."
"14764","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he said about an animal over which one brings witnesses and another brings witnesses: It belongs to the one with whom it is. He said: We said: Did he mention whether they are equal in number and probity? He said: No, only thus, as he informed us. He said: I know only that 'Ata said to me: When they are equal in probity, then the more numerous of them, unless there is doubt."
"14765","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ibn Shihab, that the more just and more numerous are followed. He said: And Ibn Shihab said about a mule belonging to a man claimed by a man as his mule, where he established ten men witnessing it was his, and the other established proof witnessing it was his and was born with him. He said: When the witnesses are equal in number, the oath is upon the defendant."
"14766","Al-Aslami reported, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that when the witnesses are equal, lots are drawn between the disputants."
"14767","Ma'mar reported, from Hammam, that he heard Abu Hurayra saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the oath to a people, and both parties hastened to swear, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that lots be drawn between them as to which of them swears."
"14768","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Hisham ibn 'Urwa informed us, from 'Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, informing them that people of Banu Sulaym disputed about a mine to Marwan ibn al-Hakam when he was governor of Madina in those days. Marwan ordered 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, who drew lots between them as to which of them swears. The lot fell upon one party, so Ibn al-Zubayr made them swear, they swore, and he judged the mine for them; that was because the witnesses were equal and he did not know by which of them to take."
"14769","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: A man informed me that people disputed about water called al-Ghubar. These brought witnesses and those brought witnesses. Mu'awiya said: What is the name? They said: Al-Ghubar. So he judged it for Ghubar, and Ghubar is a clan of Banu Yashkur."
"14770","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ibn Shihab said: If four upright witnesses testify against a woman of adultery, and four upright witnesses come and testify by Allah: She was with us on the night these saw her committing adultery, and these are lying sinners, while both parties are upright with accepted testimony, equal in probity. He said: Those who accused her are flogged, when they name one single night without differing in it."
"14771","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, who said: I asked al-Sha'bi about two men, one bringing proof that he has something due from him, and the other bringing proof that he has nothing due upon him. Sufyan said: The proof of the claimant is taken."
"14772","Ibn Jarir reported, who said: 'Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me that two clans of Arabs disputed about water. This clan brought whatever witnesses they wished, and that clan brought the like. 'Abd al-Malik said: To whom does the name belong? It was said: To Banu so-and-so, one of the two clans. So he judged it for them."
"14773","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada and Hammad, about goods found with two men both claiming them. They said: Both are made to swear; if both refuse, it is divided between them, and if both swear, it is divided between them."
"14774","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, about goods between two men, one saying: All of it is mine, the other saying: Half of it is mine. He said: For the one who said: All of it is mine, half of it, and both are made to swear, then the other half is divided between them."
"14775","From al-Thawri, about a dirham between two men, one saying: Half of it is mine, the other saying: All of it is mine. He said: As for Ibn Abi Layla, he says: A third and two thirds. As for Ibn Shubruma, he says: Three quarters and a quarter. Sufyan said: As for us, we say: It is between them in halves, and this is the most beloved view to us."
"14776","A man reported, from Hammad, about two men claiming wealth, one saying: Two thirds of it are mine, the other saying: Half of it is mine. He said: For the owner of two thirds, half; for the owner of half, a third; and they divide what remains between them."
"14777","Sufyan said, about two men from each of whom a dirham fell, and one of them found a dirham. He said: His fellow absolving him is more beloved to me; otherwise it belongs to the one in whose hands it is."
"14778","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, and from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: The house belongs to the woman except what is known to belong to the man."
"14779","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from his father, from al-Hasan, who said: For the woman is what she locked her door upon when her husband dies."
"14780","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, who said: The man has nothing except his weapon and the clothes on his body."
"14781","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, who said: Whatever new household goods the man made, if he establishes proof upon them, they are his."
"14782","From al-Thawri, from Abu Umayya 'Abd al-Karim, from Ibrahim, who said: The goods of men are for men, and the goods of women are for women; whatever is for men and women by custom is for the men, and it belongs to the survivor of them by death. Sufyan said: What we hold is that it is between them in halves."
"14783","From al-Thawri, that Shurayh said: When he places his slave in one craft then moves him to another, there is no liability upon him. Sufyan said: Our view which we hold is: When he permits him in one craft, people have been deceived by him and he is liable; it is in the slave's neck."
"14784","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Two men disputed to Shurayh; one said: I restricted my slave, then this one went and dealt with him on credit. The man said: He used to buy and sell without being stopped. He said: Your proof that he used to sell and buy; otherwise his oath by Allah that he did not permit him to sell or buy, except sending him with dirhams saying: Buy such-and-such."
"14785","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Bakkar ibn Salam, who said: A dispute was brought to 'Ali about a slave whom his master sent to buy. He said: He sent him to buy meat for a dirham, so he validated it against him. Sufyan said: And we say: If he sends him with much wealth to buy with, we say: He permitted him to trade, and people were deceived by him; but if he only sent him with one or two dirhams, it is nothing."
"14786","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, who said: When his master sends him to bring the imposed tax, he is like one permitted to trade; he holds him liable."
"14787","From al-Thawri, who said: We say: When a tax is imposed upon him, it is in the slave's neck."
"14788","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatada, who said: When a man permits his slave to marry and he marries, the dower is in the slave's neck; but if he guarantees the dower, upon him is what he guaranteed, even if more than the slave's price. Ma'mar said: And al-Zuhri said: It is upon the master when he permits him."
"14789","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, who said: When his master permits him to buy, he is liable for his debt; but when he does not permit him, it is in the slave's responsibility. He says: He is not sold. Al-Thawri reported: And our view is: He is sold."
"14790","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, who said: The debt of the slave is in his neck; it does not go beyond his saying: I have permitted you to sell him for the debt. He says: He is sold."
"14791","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan ibn Amr, from Ibrahim, he said: The slave is sold for a debt, even if it is more than his value, and he says: As they took him away, let them make him work to pay it off. Al-Thawri said: And Ibn Abi Layla said: He is not sold."
"14792","Al-Thawri reported, from Mutarrif, from al-Hakam, concerning a slave permitted to trade: He is not sold unless the debt encompasses his neck, then he is sold at that point."
"14793","Sufyan said, concerning a slave who tore the clothes of a free man: We say: If he destroys property or tears clothes, it is upon the slave's neck in the position of a debt, and if he inflicts a wound, the master is told: If you wish, hand him over for his offense, and if you wish, pay on his behalf."
"14794","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: There used to be debts in the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and we do not know of a free man sold for a debt."
"14795","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: When a man permits his slave to trade, then frees him, and it only increased him in righteousness, the creditors sell the slave as a freedman."
"14796","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a man frees his slave while a debt is upon him, the debt is upon the master."
"14797","From al-Thawri, he said: Our companions, Hammad and others, said: When he frees him while a debt is upon him, the value of the slave is upon the master, and his creditors sell him for what exceeds the value, and this is the more beloved of the two views; if anything remains beyond the value of the slave, the slave is pursued for it."
"14798","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A case was disputed before Shurayh concerning a man who sold a slave while a debt was upon the slave. He said to him: This one sold me a slave while a debt was upon him. The other said: I sold him without knowing of his debt, and I give him the choice. Shurayh said: I see your brother has given you the choice."
"14799","Ma'mar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Sale of mukhadarah was forbidden. And mukhadarah is to buy reeds for two or three cuttings before it matures, and the like of that."
"14800","And I heard other than Ma'mar narrating, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the sale of mukhadarah. And mukhadarah is the sale of fruit before it appears ripe and rosy."
"14801","Ma'mar and Muhammad ibn Muslim reported, one or both of them, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Reeds are not sold except for one cutting, nor henna, and cucumbers are not sold except for one cutting."
"14802","Ma'mar and Nu'man ibn Abi Shaybah reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said, concerning the sale of celery: He sells it as one bundle, meaning the enclosure of the harvest."
"14803","Ma'mar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: There is no harm in selling barley for fodder before its ripeness appears, when he harvests it on the spot. He said: I said to Yahya: But I neglected it until it became grain. He said: There is no harm in it."
"14804","I asked Ma'mar about two partners who divided debtors, so this one took some of them and this one took some of them, then the share of one of them perished and the share of the other was collected. He said: Al-Hasan used to say: If he acquits him of them, it is permissible."
"14805","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Whatever is collected or lost is nothing, it is between them both. Ma'mar said: And this is the more pleasing of the two views to me."
"14806","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Two partners may buy each other out."
"14807","As for Ibn Jurayj, he mentioned, from Ata, that Ibn Abbas said: There is no harm if people in a partnership buy each other out, so one of them takes ten in cash from the gold between them, and this one takes twenty dinars. Ata said: And they do not buy each other out in goods of whatever kind, except gold and silver."
"14808","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, and al-Taymi, from Yunus, from al-Hasan: They both disliked two partners and the people of inheritance buying each other out."
"14809","Al-Thawri reported, from Abu al-Zubayr, that Ibn Abbas said: There is no harm if the people of inheritance buy each other out of a debt, some of them exiting to others."
"14810","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he said concerning two partners between whom is merchandise or goods that are not measured nor weighed: There is no harm if one of them buys it from the other."
"14811","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, he said: Any woman settled with for her eighth without her husband's inheritance being clarified to her, that is all doubt."
"14812","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Amr ibn Dinar: That the wife of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, his family bought her out of a third of the eighth for eighty-three thousand dirhams."
"14813","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would not pray over a man upon whom was a debt. A dead man was brought, so he asked: Is there a debt upon him. They said: Yes, two dinars. He said: Pray over your companion. Abu Qatadah said: They are upon me, O Messenger of Allah. So he prayed over him. Then when Allah granted victory to His Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: I am closer to every believer than his own self; whoever leaves a debt, it is upon me, and whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs."
"14814","Abdullah ibn Umar reported, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was brought the bier of a man from my people to pray over him. He said: Is there a debt upon your companion. They said: Yes, upon him is a dozen or so dirhams. He said: Pray over your companion. I said: It is upon me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: So he prayed over him."
"14815","Ja'far ibn Sulayman reported, he said: Asma ibn Ubayd narrated to me that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, met Abu Qatadah after that and said: Did you pay on behalf of your companion. He said: I am engaged in it, O Messenger of Allah. Then a second time, then a third time, and he said: I have finished it, O Messenger of Allah. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Now you have cooled your companion's resting place."
"14816","Ibrahim ibn Yazid reported, he said: Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: When a bier was brought to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for him to pray over it, he said: Is there a debt upon your companion. If they said yes, he said: Did he leave fulfillment. If they said yes, he prayed over him, and if they said no, he did not pray over him. Then a man was brought, and he asked this question, and they said no. He said: Pray over your companion. Then his cousin said: His debt is upon me. So he prayed over him. Then he said: O Banu Salamah, would you like to admit your companion into Paradise. They said: What would we do, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You settle his debt. He said: I think he said: So they did, and they said: It is only two dinars."
"14817","Ma'mar reported, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I am the closest of people to the believers in the Book of Allah; so whichever of you leaves a debt or helpless dependents, call me, for I am his guardian, and whichever of you leaves wealth, let him favor his blood relatives with his wealth."
"14818","From al-Thawri, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his family, and whoever leaves a debt or neglected dependents, it is to me and upon me, for I am the closest to the believers."
"14819","From al-Thawri, he said: My father narrated to us, from Sam'an ibn Mushannaj, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at a funeral. He said: Is there anyone here from Banu so-and-so. No one answered him. Then he said: Is there anyone here from Banu so-and-so. No one answered him. Then he said: Is there anyone here from Banu so-and-so. Then a man stood and said: I am, O Messenger of Allah. He said: What prevented you from answering me the first two times. I intended nothing for you but good. Your companion is held back by his debt. I saw him pay on his behalf until no one remained demanding him for anything."
"14820","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Musa ibn Abi Isa, or another, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab removed a spout belonging to al-Abbas over the mosque. Al-Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is the one who placed it with his own hand. Umar said: There shall be no ladder for you to it except my back. He said: So Umar bent down for him, al-Abbas mounted upon his back, and fixed it."
"14821","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever removes anything from its boundary and it hits something, he is liable."
"14822","Ibn Mujahid reported, from his father, that Ali said: Whoever digs a well or lays out a timber and it hits a person, he is liable."
"14823","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Asim, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Shurayh had no spout except inside his own house."
"14824","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from his father, from Khaythamah, that he said concerning a man who buys something for a man for a dirham, then obtains an increase: The increase belongs to the owner of the dirham."
"14825","Al-Thawri reported: When you buy a purchase and then obtain an increase, then find a defect in the purchase and return it, return the increase and the purchase together, unless he wishes to hand over the increase to you."
"14826","Qays ibn al-Rabi reported, from Mansur, he said: I asked Ibrahim about a man who buys something for two dirhams, fresh dates or otherwise, and eats from it while measuring. He said: There is no harm in it."
"14827","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, concerning a man who contracts with a man for work, and he does part of it, then a flood comes and carries it away or ruins it. He said: He works for him to the extent of what remains of his work. Ma'mar said: And I asked Ibn Shubrumah about it, and he said: He is given according to what he did."
"14828","I asked Ma'mar about a man who contracts with a man to dig him a well until its water appears, and he digs in it for days, then meets rock and cannot dig. Qatadah said: He has nothing."
"14829","Sa'id ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Yasar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Asim informed me that his sister said to him: I want you to buy specified goods for me, so seek it for me. He said: I said: I have food with me. So I sold her food for gold on credit, and she took full possession of it. Then she said: Look for one who will buy it from me. I said: I will sell it for you. He said: So I sold it for her, and something of doubt fell into my mind. So I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, and he said: See that you are not its owner. He said: I said: I am its owner. He said: That is sheer usury, so take your capital and return the excess to her."
"14830","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from Abu Ka'b, he said: I said to al-Hasan: I sell silk, and a woman and a Bedouin buy from me, saying: Sell it for us, for you know the market better. Al-Hasan said: Do not sell it, nor buy it, nor guide him, except that you guide him to the market."
"14831","Ja'far reported, from Ruzayq ibn Abi Salma, he said: I asked al-Hasan about selling silk. He said: Sell, and fear Allah. He said: He sells it for himself. He said: When you have bought it, do not point anyone to it, nor be involved in it in anything; hand over his goods to him and leave him."
"14832","I asked al-Thawri, from al-Shaybani, about a man upon whom is a debt to his wife or to another, then he pays a favored child of his whose property is entrusted, with a debt owed to them, then the others demand. He said: When he pays them while healthy, it is permissible for them, even if there is a debt upon him to others."
"14833","From Ma'mar, he said: When he pays them something while they are minors, they have the choice when they grow up."
"14834","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: Whatever a man settles in his child's property is valid, and whatever a child settles in his father's property is not valid."
"14835","Al-Thawri reported, from Sulayman, from al-Sha'bi, that he said, concerning a man who consumes another man's wheat: Its owner is owed food like his food, measure for measure. Sufyan said: And others among our jurists used to say: He is owed the value. And the view of al-Sha'bi is dearer to Sufyan."
"14836","Ma'mar reported, from a man who asked al-Hakam ibn Utaybah about a man who burns something on his land with fire, and the blaze flies and the fire spreads to another, and burns something on his neighbor's land. He said: There is no liability upon him; nothing is upon him."
"14837","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman: That Umar disliked taking a stipend for judiciary, and for the custodian of their spoils."
"14838","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam: That Umar ibn al-Khattab provided Shurayh and Salman ibn Rabi'ah al-Bahili a stipend for judiciary."
"14839","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Masruq did not take a stipend for judiciary, and Shurayh took."
"14840","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, son of Masruq's brother, from his father, from Masruq: That he would not take a stipend for judiciary, and when an expedition went out, he would appoint a substitute for himself."
"14841","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Four things have no stipend taken for them: judiciary, the call to prayer, the divider, he said, and I think he mentioned the Qur'an."
"14842","Ma'mar reported, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: A man should not be a judge until five things are in him; whichever of them misses him, it is a defect in him: He is knowledgeable of what came before him, consults the people of knowledge, rejects bribery, meaning greed, forbearing toward litigants, enduring blame."
"14843","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Amr ibn Amir, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: A man should not be a judge until five traits are in him; if one trait misses him, it is a blemish in him: That he is knowledgeable of what came before him, consults men of judgment, restrained from greed, forbearing toward litigants, enduring blame."
"14844","Yahya ibn al-Ala reported, from Abdullah ibn Imran, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: None should undertake this affair, meaning the affair of the people, except a man in whom are four qualities: Gentleness without weakness, firmness without violence, restraint without miserliness, and generosity without waste. If one of them falls, the other three are corrupted."
"14845","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Mis'ar, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: None upholds the command of Allah except one who does not flatter, nor imitate, nor follow greed. And none upholds the command of Allah except a man who speaks a word with his tongue that does not diminish his vigor, and does not covet the truth against its sharpness. He says: He does not covet, lest he weaken."
"14846","Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah informed me, from Abu Hariz who was in Sijistan, he said: Umar wrote to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari: Do not sell, nor buy, nor consult in private, nor harm, nor take a bribe in judgment, nor judge between two while you are angry."
"14847","Yahya ibn al-Ala reported, from Ismail ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, he said: A guest stayed with Ali ibn Abi Talib, and was with him for days. Then he came in a dispute. Ali said to him: Are you a disputant. He said: Yes. He said: Then depart from us, for we are forbidden to host a disputant except with his opponent."
"14848","Sufyan said: The judge, his entire sitting is impartial."
"14849","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Umar said to Abu Musa: It has reached me that you judge while you are not a commander. He said: Yes. He said: Then undertake its heat who undertakes its cool."
"14850","Al-Thawri reported, from Asim, concerning two men who came to Ubaydah disputing to him. He said: Do you appoint me. They said: Yes. So he judged between them. Sufyan said: And when two men appoint an arbiter and he judges between them, his judgment is valid, except in the fixed punishments."
"14851","Ma'mar reported, from al-Mas'udi, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Ibn Mas'ud said: When a matter comes to you from which you find no escape, judge by what is in the Book of Allah. If you are unable, judge by the Sunnah of the Prophet of Allah. If you are unable, judge by what the righteous judged. If you are unable, gesture a gesture, and do not fall short. If you are unable, flee from it and do not be shy."
"14852","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Give judgments and have progeny."
"14853","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I heard Shurayh saying: I do not reverse a judgment that came before me."
"14854","From al-Thawri, he said: When a judge judges contrary to the Book of Allah, or the Sunnah of the Prophet of Allah, or something agreed upon, the judge after him reverses it. If it was something by the opinion of the people, he does not reverse it, and he bears what it entails."
"14855","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not appoint a judge until he died, nor Abu Bakr, nor Umar, except that he said to a man at the end of his caliphate: Suffice me some of the affairs of the people, meaning Ali."
"14856","I heard more than one mentioning that Uthman sent Zayd ibn Thabit over judiciary."
"14857","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A man confessed before Shurayh about a matter, then denied it. So he judged against him by his confession. He said: Do you judge against me without proof. He said: The son of your maternal aunt's sister testified against you."
"14858","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Awn, from Ibrahim, he said: Shurayh judged against a man by his confession. He said: O Abu Umayyah, you judged against me without proof. He said: The son of your maternal aunt's sister informed me."
"14859","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: We heard that the arbiter, his entire word is valid in confession between the two disputants, except in the fixed punishments."
"14860","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Muharib ibn Dithar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Turn back the disputants until they reconcile, for decisive judgment breeds hatred between people. Sufyan said: But we apply this when there is doubt and kinship. As for when the judgment is clear to him, he should not turn them back."
"14861","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: It is not lawful for the Imam to reconcile between them when the judgment is clear to him. And Ma'mar said it, from Ibn Abi Layla."
"14862","From al-Thawri, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I heard Shurayh saying: No judgment is given against one absent."
"14863","Muhammad ibn Muslim reported, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said, Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said, Luqman said: When a man comes to you with his eyes fallen out, do not judge for him until his opponent comes. He said: He says: Perhaps he will come having torn out four eyes."
"14864","Al-Thawri said, concerning a man who deputes a man to seek a right of his from an absent man, and the one claimed from says: I paid to your companion. He said: Do not pay him anything until the principal owner arrives, then he swears he collected nothing from him."
"14865","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I witnessed Shurayh while a man disputed to him about a debt he sought with a delay. Another said: He excuses his companion; he is insolvent. Allah the Exalted says: And if one is in hardship, respite until ease. Shurayh said: This was concerning usury, and usury was among the Ansar. Allah says: Render trusts to their owners, and when you judge between people, judge justly. No, by Allah, Allah does not command a command that you oppose. Detain him beside this pillar until he pays him."
"14866","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When Shurayh judged a right against a man, he would detain him in the mosque until he stood. If he gave him his right, otherwise he ordered him to prison."
"14867","Waki reported, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from Umm Ja'far, a concubine of al-Sha'bi, she said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: If I do not detain for a debt, I waste his right."
"14868","Waki said: And al-Hasan ibn Salih informed me, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Detention for a debt is life."
"14869","He said: And Jabir said: Ali would detain for a debt."
"14870","Ma'mar reported, from Bahz ibn Hakim ibn Mu'awiyah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, detained a man for a while on suspicion, then released him."
"14871","Al-Nu'man ibn Abi Hanifah and Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When a man does not acknowledge the judgment, he is detained."
"14872","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Mas'ud, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would be brought captives from the fifth, and would give a household all together, and disliked separating them. Ma'mar said in his hadith: And he sent to Ibn Mas'ud a household."
"14873","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Hasan, from his mother Fatimah bint Husayn, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Zayd ibn Harithah on an expedition, and he took captives and brought them. He needed mounts, so he sold a boy among them. His mother came, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw her weeping. So he asked him, and he said: I needed some mounts, so I sold her son. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Return and take him back or buy him. He said: So he later gifted him to Ali. He said: So he was a treasurer for him. He said: And he bore him a child."
"14874","Ibn Jurayj said, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, that Abu Usayd came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with captives from al-Bahrayn. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, looked at a woman among them weeping. He said: What is your matter. She said: He sold my son. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Abu Usayd: Did you sell her son. He said: Yes. He said: To whom. He said: To Banu Abs. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Ride yourself and bring him."
"14875","Ma'mar reported, from Jabir, from a man he named, that Ibn Umar: A slave girl from al-Basrah was bought for him. When she entered upon him, she wept. He said: What is your matter. She said: I remembered my father. So Ibn Umar freed her."
"14876","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Farrukh, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: That two brothers are not separated when sold. Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Amr, from Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: He wrote the like of it exactly."
"14877","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Hakim ibn Iqal, or another, that Uthman ibn Affan ordered him to buy slaves for him, and said: Do not separate a mother and her child."
"14878","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike separating a man and his child, a woman and her child, and brothers. Mansur said: So I said to Ibrahim: But you sold a slave girl while her mother was with you. He said: I placed her in a righteous place, and she gave permission for that."
"14879","Ma'mar reported, from Hammad, he said: I said to Ibrahim: Did they dislike separating a mother and her child. He said: Yes. They disliked trade in slaves only for that."
"14880","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That he used to dislike separating the captives who were brought."
"14881","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That he bought a native-born slave girl from some of the people of Makkah, while her father was alive, then went out with her to al-Janad."
"14882","Ma'mar reported, from al-Sha'bi, he said: There is no harm if a native-born girl is sold, even if her mother dislikes it, when the girl has matured and is independent of her mother."
"14883","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: The sale of a boy is not valid until he reaches puberty."
"14884","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Amir and Ibrahim, they said: The sale of a boy and his purchase are not valid until he reaches puberty."
"14885","Isra'il reported, from Jabir, from Amir, he said: The sale of a boy is not valid until he understands."
"14886","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: If you find sound judgment in them, he said: Intellect."
"14887","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A guardian sold a slave and maid of a girl under his care, and she disputed about it to Shurayh. Shurayh said to the witnesses: Do you testify that she gave permission and accepted. They said: No. Until one of them passed by him, and he said: Do you testify that she gave permission and accepted. He said: Rather I testify that she shouted and wept. She remained that day in the sun, and I testify that he sold over her while compelled. He said: So he upheld the sale over her."
"14888","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A man came to Shurayh disputing a woman. He said: She overcharged me. Shurayh said: That is what she intended. He said: And he used to reverse a mistake."
"14889","Isra'il reported, from Jabir, or another, from Amir, concerning a man who bought a garment from a man and said: I made a mistake. He said: It is nothing; sale is shrewdness. He said: And al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman used to reverse a mistake."
"14890","Ma'mar was asked about two men completing a sale, and one of them claims he made a mistake. He said: It reached me from more than one that if he brings a clear matter, it is reversed, and if he does not bring a clear matter, it is enforced against him."
"14891","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: The sale of one intoxicated and his purchase and his marriage are not valid."
"14892","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from Muslim ibn al-Dayyal, he said: I asked Ibn Shubrumah about the sale and purchase of one intoxicated. He said: It is not valid when it is known he does not understand. He said: And his divorce is valid. As for his marriage, I do not know; perhaps it is not valid. He said: And I asked Ibn Abi Layla, and he said: As for his divorce and marriage, they are valid, and as for sale and purchase, it is not valid when he does not understand."
"14893","Al-Thawri reported, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, he said: A man asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Prophet of Allah, I am deceived in sale. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: To whomsoever you trade, say: No deceit, meaning no treachery."
"14894","Al-Thawri reported, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with deafness in his ears. He said: A man comes to me whispering one thing to me, and announces other than that, and I do not hear him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: To whomsoever you trade, say: I sell you for such and such, and no concealment."
"14895","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Awn, he said: Cloth used to come to me from the land of Persia, and I also bought from al-Basrah. People would come to me and say: Do you have such-and-such cloth. I would bring out for them from what came to me and from what I bought from al-Basrah, and they would not ask me, nor would I inform them, except that I think they suppose it is from what comes to me. He said: So I asked Ibn Sirin, and he said: Deceit. Ma'mar said: So I mentioned it to Ayyub, and he said: This does not please me."
"14896","Ma'mar was asked about a man with whom a man deposited a Nabati load, then he came to him afterward, and he gave him a Sabiri load, erring with it, and part of it perished. He said: So he is liable."
"14897","Ma'mar reported, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Wahb, he said: A Bedouin brought sheep. Mu'adh ibn Jabal passed by him and bargained with him. The Bedouin swore he would not sell it to him for that. Then the Bedouin passed by him after that and said to Mu'adh: Do you want it. He said: For how much. He said: For the price you offered me. Mu'adh said: I would not make you sin."
"14898","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, concerning a man who bargains with a man for goods, and swears he will not sell it to him for that price, then it appears to him afterward to sell it for that price to the one he swore not to sell it to. He said: There is no harm if he buys it from him for that, and the sin is upon the one who swore."
"14899","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cursed the eater of usury, its giver, its witness, and its scribe."
"14900","Ma'mar reported, from Ata al-Khurasani, from a man, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: Usury is seventy-three sins; the least of them in sin is like one who comes to his mother in Islam, and a dirham of usury is like thirty-odd adulteries."
"14901","Umar ibn Rashid reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man of the Ansar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Usury is seventy-one, or he said: Seventy-three sins; the least is like a man coming to his mother, and the worst usury is a man extending in the honor of his Muslim brother."
"14902","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umarah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Usury is seventy-odd doors; the easiest is like one who comes to his mother in Islam."
"14903","Al-Thawri reported, from Zubayd, from Ibrahim, from Masruq, from Abdullah, he said: Usury is seventy-odd doors, and association with Allah is similar to that."
"14904","Bakkar reported, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrating, from Abdullah ibn Hanzalah, from Ka'b, that he said: That I commit thirty-three adulteries is dearer to me than that I eat a dirham of usury, Allah knowing that I ate it when I ate it while it is usury. From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdullah ibn Hanzalah, from Ka'b, the like of it."
"14905","Ma'mar reported, from al-A'mash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The eater of usury and its giver and its two witnesses, when they know of it, and the woman who joins hair and the one who seeks it, and the muhallil and the one for whom it is done, and the withholder of charity and the transgressor in it, and the apostate upon his heels as a Bedouin after his migration, are cursed upon the tongue of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the Day of Resurrection."
"14906","Ma'mar reported, from Shu'ayb ibn al-Habhab, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cursed the eater of usury and its giver and its two witnesses and its scribe, and the tattooing woman and the one tattooed for beauty, and the withholder of charity, and the muhallil and the one for whom it is done, and he used to forbid wailing. Al-Thawri reported, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi and al-Harith, from Ali, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"14907","Ma'mar reported, he said: We heard that forty years do not pass over the man of usury until he is wiped out. And al-Thawri said it also. Abd al-Razzaq said: I have seen it."
"14908","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Musa ibn Abdillah, from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, that he sent a slave of his with four thousand to Isfahan. Then it reached him that he died, so he rode to him or sent to him, and found the wealth had reached twenty-four thousand. It was said to him: He used to deal with the wealth for usury. So he took four thousand, his capital, and left twenty thousand. It was said to him: Take it. He said: It is not mine. It was said: Gift it to us. So he left it and did not take it."
"14909","Ma'mar reported, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Part of injustice is the delay of the rich, and when one of you is transferred to a solvent one, let him follow. Ma'mar said: And a man added for me in this hadith, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: And the most lying of people are the craftsmen."
"14910","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Dhakwan, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Delay is the injustice of the rich, and whoever is transferred to a solvent one, let him follow."
"14911","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sinan, from a man who heard Abu Hurayrah saying: Whoever has a debt upon him, and becomes able for it but does not settle it, he is like the eater of illicit gain."
"14912","Ma'mar reported, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, bought a camel from a Bedouin for a wasq of dates. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asked him for respite to a named term. The Bedouin said: O treachery. The companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, were about to attack him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Leave him, for the owner of a right has a say. Take him to so-and-so, a woman of the Ansar, and order her to pay him. She said: I have nothing except dates better than his right. He said: Let her pay him, and feed him. So she did. The Bedouin passed by the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: May Allah reward you well; you paid and paid well. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Those are the best of people, those who pay and pay well."
"14913","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Mis'ar, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, paid me and increased me."
"14914","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic commanded just witnesses, and recited: Those who buy the covenant of Allah and their oaths for a small price, the verse. So a man should look at what he testifies and understand."
"14915","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, he said: I said to Ibrahim: Who is upright among the Muslims. He said: Those in whom no suspicion has appeared."
"14916","Ma'mar reported, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from his father, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: None is allowed among witnesses except the upright, unsuspected. For it reached us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The testimony of a treacherous man, nor a treacherous woman, nor one with hatred for his brother, nor an innovator in Islam, nor a female innovator, is not valid."
"14917","Al-Aslami reported, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The testimony of a treacherous man, nor a treacherous woman, nor one with hatred against his brother, nor an innovator in Islam, nor a female innovator, is not valid."
"14918","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The testimony of a treacherous man, nor a treacherous woman, nor one with hatred against his brother, is not valid. And the testimony of the dependent for the household is not valid, and his testimony for others is valid. He said: And the dependent is the follower upon whom the household spends."
"14919","Al-Aslami reported, from Abdullah, from Yazid ibn Talhah, from Talhah ibn Abdillah ibn Awf, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a caller in the market that the testimony of an adversary, nor a suspect, is not valid. It was said: And what is the suspect. He said: The accused in his religion."
"14920","Al-Thawri reported, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Hakam ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Farrukh, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: The testimony of one with suspicion, nor enmity, nor insanity, is not valid."
"14921","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: Allah and His Messenger decreed: The testimony of a treacherous man, nor a treacherous woman, nor an adversary with rancor in the heart of his companion, is not valid."
"14922","Al-Thawri reported, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: He used to say: The testimony of an accused, nor a suspect in divorce, is not valid."
"14923","Al-Thawri reported, from Ismail, from a man: That a man testified before Shurayh, and he judged for his companion. The one judged against stood to inform the judge. The witness pulled him. So Shurayh nullified his testimony."
"14924","Al-Thawri reported, from al-A'mash, from Umarah, or from Yahya, that a man testified before Shurayh wearing a tailored qaba. Shurayh said: Do you pray well. He said: Yes. He said: Do you perform ablution well. He said: Yes. He said: How do you perform ablution. He went to take his hands out of the sleeves and could not. So he did not validate his testimony."
"14925","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, he said: I heard Shurayh saying: I do not validate against you the testimony of an adversary, nor a partner, nor a repeller of loss, nor a bringer of gain, nor a suspicious one. He said: Then he says: And you, ask about him. If they say: Allah knows best of him, then Allah knows best of him, and his testimony is not valid, for they fear to disparage him. If they say: Upright, as far as we know, pleasing, his testimony is valid."
"14926","Al-Thawri reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: When a man impugns the witness, he says: I do not validate against you the testimony of an adversary, nor a repeller of loss, nor slaves, nor a hireling, nor a partner. And you, ask. If it is said: Allah knows best of him, then Allah knows best of him; they feared to say: Suspicious, so his testimony is not valid. If it is said: We know him only as upright, a Muslim, then he is, God willing, as they said."
"14927","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: Is the testimony of the blind allowed. He said: Yes. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to appoint Ibn Umm Maktum over al-Madinah over the weak when he traveled, and he would lead them in prayer."
"14928","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: The testimony of the blind is valid when he is pleasing."
"14929","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, he said: The testimony of the blind is valid in rights."
"14930","Isra'il reported, from Simak, he said: Isa informed me, he said: I saw al-Sha'bi validate the testimony of a blind man."
"14931","Al-Thawri reported, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to validate the testimony of the blind in a trifling matter."
"14932","Abu Sufyan reported, he said: Ibn Layla used to validate the testimony of the blind."
"14933","Al-Thawri reported, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: He used to dislike the testimony of the blind."
"14934","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from their elders, that Ali did not validate the testimony of a blind man in theft."
"14935","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata said to me: The child of adultery, when nothing is known of him but good, his testimony is valid."
"14936","Al-Thawri reported from Zuhayr ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard al-Shabi saying: the testimony of the child of adultery is valid."
"14937","Mamar and al-Thawri reported from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from Shurayh, he said: the testimony of the slave for his master is not valid, nor of the hired man for the one who hired him, nor of the partner; Mamar said in his narration: Shurayh used to allow the testimony of the slave in a small matter."
"14938","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ayyub informed me, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: the testimony of the slave for his master is not valid, nor of the hired man for the one who hired him."
"14939","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Mazini reported from al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: the testimony of the master for his slave is not valid, nor of the slave for his master, nor of a partner for his partner concerning the thing shared between them; as for anything besides that, his testimony is valid."
"14940","Al-Thawri reported from a man he named, from Amir, he said: I saw Shurayh before whom a slave testified concerning a house, and he allowed his testimony; it was said to him: he is a slave; he said: we are all slaves."
"14941","Israel reported from Isa ibn Abi Azza, he said: I heard Amir saying: no testimony of a slave is valid."
"14942","Abu Sufyan reported from Shuba, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab make a false witness stand in the evening in a waist-wrapper, rebuking himself."
"14943","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: Shurayh made the false witness stand upon an elevated place."
"14944","Qays ibn al-Rabi reported from Abu Husayn, he said: Abdullah ibn Utba, when he seized a false witness, if he was an Arab he sent him to the mosque of his people, and if he was a client he sent him to his market, and said: we found this one a false witness, and we do not accept his testimony."
"14945","Al-Thawri reported from al-Jad ibn Dhakwan, he said: Shurayh was brought a false witness, so he pulled off his turban, struck him blows with the stick, and sent him to the mosque so the people would know him."
"14946","I said to Muhammad ibn Rashid: did you hear Makhul narrating, from al-Walid ibn Abi Malik, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to his governors in Syria concerning the false witness: that he be flogged forty lashes, his face blackened, his head shaved, and his imprisonment prolonged; he said: No, but al-Hajjaj ibn Artat mentioned it from him; and Yahya ibn al-Ala informed us that he heard al-Hajjaj narrating, from Makhul, from al-Walid, from Umar, the same."
"14947","Yahya ibn al-Ala reported, he said: al-Ahwas ibn Hakim informed me, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered concerning the false witness that his face be blackened, his turban be thrown around his neck, he be paraded among the tribes, and it be said: this is a false witness, so do not accept testimony from him."
"14948","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Bahdala, from a man he named, I think he said: Wail ibn Rabia, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Masud saying: false testimony was equated with associating partners with Allah; then Abdullah recited this verse: So avoid the filth of idols and avoid false speech."
"14949","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I was told from Makhul that Umar ibn al-Khattab beat a false witness forty lashes."
"14950","Al-Thawri reported from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: people testified before Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz concerning the sighting of the crescent, so he nullified their testimony and beat them."
"14951","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to him, meaning Ata: a man stole and his hand was cut, then he repented and good was said of him, is his testimony valid; he said: Yes; I said to him: a man flogged for wine, then good is praised of him; he said: his testimony is valid."
"14952","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Muhammad ibn Kurdus, from Shurayh, he said: a man who had been beaten for wine testified before him, so he said: what do you know of him; Kurdus said: he is among the righteous of our youth, so he allowed his testimony."
"14953","Israel reported from Isa ibn Abi Azza, he said: I saw Amir allow the testimony of a man punished for wine, and he said: when he repents we accept his testimony."
"14954","Al-Thawri reported from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, he said: the testimony of women along with men is valid in marriage and divorce."
"14955","Mamar reported from al-Hasan and al-Zuhri, they said: the testimony of women is not valid in a hadd punishment, nor divorce, nor marriage, even if a man is with them."
"14956","Mamar reported from Qatada, he said: the testimony of women is not valid in divorce nor marriage."
"14957","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Ibrahim, he said: the testimony of women along with men is not valid in divorce and marriage."
"14958","Al-Hasan ibn Umara reported from al-Hakam ibn Utayba that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: the testimony of women is not valid in divorce, marriage, hadd punishments, and blood cases."
"14959","He said: al-Hakam and Mansur informed me, from Ibrahim, he said: if a Companion of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and two women testified before me in a divorce, I would not allow it; Mamar said: I heard al-Zuhri narrating, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar, the same as the saying of Ali."
"14960","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported, he said: I heard Makhul saying: the testimony of women is not valid except in debt."
"14961","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: the testimony of women along with men is not valid except in manumission, debt, and bequest."
"14962","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abd al-Rahman, he said: the testimony of women is not valid in hadd punishments."
"14963","From al-Thawri, from Bayan, from Ibrahim, concerning three who testified along with two women, he said: No, except four men, or they are flogged."
"14964","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: the testimony of women is not valid in hadd punishments, nor of a man testifying to the testimony of a man, nor is a man taken as surety in a hadd case."
"14965","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Hujayr informed me, from one he approves of, meaning Tawus, that the testimony of women along with men is valid in everything except adultery, because they should not look at that, while a man should come upon that to establish it."
"14966","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, he said: the testimony of women along with men is valid in everything, and against adultery two women along with three men who saw of it is valid."
"14967","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Allah commanded in debt the testimony of two men, and if there are not two men then a man and two women, and He did not forbid the testimony of women along with men in that; so he held that the testimony of women is valid along with the testimony of one just man in bequest; and Ibn Shihab said: the testimony of women is valid for killing if one man is with them."
"14968","Al-Aslami informed me, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artat informed me, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab allowed the testimony of one man along with women in a marriage."
"14969","Abu Sufyan reported from Ibn Awn, from al-Shabi, that Shurayh allowed the testimony of two women in a manumission."
"14970","From al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Umar ibn al-Khattab did not take the testimony of a woman in nursing; he said: Ibn Abi Layla did not take the testimony of a woman in nursing."
"14971","Al-Aslami reported from Ibn Damra, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, he said: the testimony of women alone is not valid, even for a dirham, until a man is with them."
"14972","Ibn Jurayj reported from Abu al-Zinad, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: the testimony of women is not valid when no man is with them."
"14973","Mamar reported from Qatada, he said: the testimony of women is not valid unless they are four."
"14974","Abdullah reported from Shuba, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: I heard al-Shabi saying: what is valid of the testimony of women concerning what men do not see is four; Shuba said: I asked al-Hakam about it and he said: two; I asked Hammad and he said: one."
"14975","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi and al-Hasan, they said: the testimony of one woman is valid in what men do not observe."
"14976","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: the testimony of a woman alone is valid in the newborns cry at birth."
"14977","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabra reported from Musa ibn Uqba, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Ibn Umar, he said: the testimony of women is not valid except concerning what none but they observe of the private matters of women, and what resembles that of their pregnancy and menstruation; Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abu Bakr informed me that Amr ibn Sulaym, their freedman, narrated to them the same as this narration of Ibn Umar, from Ibn al-Musayyib; he said: and he narrated to me from Abu al-Nadr, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, the same, and from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, the same."
"14978","Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Shihab said: the Sunna has passed that the testimony of women without a man among them is valid in what they handle of a womans delivery, the newborns cry, and other affairs of women which none observes or handles but they; so when a Muslim woman accepted by women, what is more than one woman, testifies in the newborns cry, it is valid."
"14979","Hisham reported from al-Hasan, he said: the testimony of one woman is valid in the newborns cry."
"14980","Al-Aslami reported, he said: Ishaq informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Umar ibn al-Khattab allowed the testimony of a woman in the newborns cry."
"14981","Al-Thawri reported from Abd al-Ala, from Shurayh: he allowed the testimony of the midwife alone in the newborns cry."
"14982","Al-Thawri reported from Abdullah, from Shurayh: he allowed the testimony of the midwife alone; al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, the same."
"14983","Mamar reported from al-Hasan and al-Zuhri, they said: the testimony of one woman is valid in nursing."
"14984","Ibn Jurayj and Mamar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: Uthman separated members of households by the testimony of a woman."
"14985","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Ubayd ibn Abi Maryam, from Uqba ibn al-Harith, he said: Ibn Abi Mulayka said, and I heard it from Uqba also, he said: a woman married at the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then a black slave-girl came and claimed she had nursed them both, so I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned that to him, and I said: she is lying; he said: how do you deal with the saying of this one; leave her; Mamar said: and I heard him saying: how are you when it has been said."
"14986","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Abi Mulayka informed me that Uqba ibn al-Harith informed him, or he heard it from him if he did not specify it to him, that he married Umm Yahya bint Abi Ihab, then a black woman said: I nursed you both; he said: so I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned that to him, and he turned away from me; then I came to him a second time and mentioned that to him, so he said: how, when she claimed she nursed you both; so he forbade him from her."
"14987","A shaykh from the people of Najran reported, he said: I heard Ibn al-Baylamani narrating, from his father, from Ibn Umar, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked: what witnesses are valid in nursing; he said: a man and a woman."
"14988","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: judges used to separate couples by the testimony of a woman in nursing."
"14989","Mamar reported from Qatada, from Abu al-Shatha, from Ibn Abbas, he said: the testimony of one woman is valid in nursing when she is trustworthy, and she is made to swear with her testimony; and he used to join this narration, and I do not know whether it is from the narration of Qatada or not; and a man came to Ibn Abbas and said: so-and-so claimed she nursed me and my wife, while she is lying; Ibn Abbas said: see, if she is lying then calamity will befall her; a year did not pass until her breasts became leprous."
"14990","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Mulayka informed me that Alqama ibn Waqqas informed him that Umm Salama, the wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, testified for Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Zuhayr and his brothers that Rabia ibn Umayya took his share from Rabia; none but she testified to that, so Muawiya allowed her testimony alone, while Alqama was present at all of that judgment of Muawiya; he said: Khalid ibn Muhammad ibn Abdullah informed me that Muawiyas messenger in that to Umm Salama was al-Harith, and Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr."
"14991","Ibn Abi Mulayka informed me, he said: the sons of Suhayb, the freedman of Ibn Judan, claimed two houses and a room, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave that to Suhayb; Marwan said: who testifies for you to that; he said: Ibn Umar; so he called him and he testified that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave Suhayb two houses and a room; so Marwan judged for them by his testimony."
"14992","Waki reported from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: I testified before Zurara ibn Abi Awfa, and he allowed my testimony, and evil is what he did."
"14993","And I testified before Mutarrif ibn Mazin, and he allowed my testimony alone."
"14994","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabra and Yahya ibn Said said: the testimony of one trustworthy woman is valid in the newborns cry."
"14995","Ibn al-Taymi reported from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: in nursing the testimony of one woman is not valid."
"14996","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me, from Abu al-Zinad: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah and His Messenger decreed; then he mentioned chapters of testimony, placing them in their places in adultery and other matters; then he said: for wine there are two witnesses, then its drinker is flogged, deprived, and harmed until repentance becomes clear from him; he said: for a debt there are two witnesses, then his right is executed for him; but if one just witness testifies, the owner of the right swears along with his witness when he is just."
"14997","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: the testimony of a man for a man in rights is valid; and Shurayh says to the witness: say: a just person made me a witness."
"14998","Al-Thawri reported from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from Shurayh, he said: his companions knew what he says, so he would say to the witness when he came testifying to the testimony of a man: say: two just persons made me a witness; and when the witness came and said: I testify by the testimony of Allah, he would say: testify by your own testimony, for Allah testifies only to the truth."
"14999","Mamar reported from a man, from Ibrahim, he said: the testimony of a man for a man in rights is valid."
"15000","Al-Aslami reported from Husayn ibn Damira, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, he said: concerning the testimony of a dead person, only two men are valid."
"15001","Mamar reported from Qatada, he said: the testimony of a man for a man in hadd punishments is not valid."
"15002","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Shihab said: judges nullified the testimony of the dead, unless the claimant brings witnesses to the testimony of the dead."
"15003","Al-Thawri reported from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, he said: no testimony upon testimony is valid in a hadd case, nor is surety taken in a hadd case."
"15004","Israel reported from Jabir, from Amir, he said: Shurayh and Masruq did not allow testimony upon testimony in a hadd case, nor did they stand surety for a person subject to a hadd."
"15005","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ismail said to me: the ruler does not act upon his own testimony; Ibn Jurayj said: and I myself hold the saying of Ata concerning the sighting of the crescent: one man, and the saying of Umar and Uthman concerning it."
"15006","Mamar and al-Thawri reported from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ikrima the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: what if I saw a man commit adultery or steal; he said: I consider your testimony the testimony of a man among the Muslims; he said: you are right."
"15007","Muhammad ibn Muslim reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yahya ibn Jada, that Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man stealing a cup, so he said: does this one not feel shame to come with a vessel he carries on his neck on the Day of Resurrection."
"15008","Ibn Uyayna reported from Ibn Shubruma, from al-Shabi, he said: I said to him: O Abu Amr, what about two men called to witness a testimony, then one of them dies and the other seeks judgment; he said: Shurayh was brought such a case while I was sitting, and he said: bring the commander and I will testify for you."
"15009","Al-Thawri and Mamar reported from Ibn Shubruma, from al-Shabi, he said: a man made Shurayh a witness, then he came disputing to him, so he said: bring the commander, and I will testify for you."
"15010","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abdullah ibn Amr informed us that Alqama ibn Nadla and Muadh ibn Uthman disputed to Abd al-Malik in his caliphate, and Abd al-Malik had testimony for Alqama; Alqama said: Abd al-Malik said: I have testimony for you, so if you wish I will testify; Muadh said: testify, O Abd al-Malik; when he testified I said: judge by your knowledge; he said: No, now I am a witness, not a judge between you; had I not testified I would have judged; he said: Muadh ibn Uthman wanted that."
"15011","Ibn Jurayj said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Malik in his caliphate saw a slave-boy of his, or one of his family, committing adultery with a woman of his slave-girls or others of their family, and intended to apply the hadd upon him, so Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz forbade him from acting upon his own testimony until four testify."
"15012","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I was told that Umar wrote to Abu Musa: the ruler does not act upon his knowledge, nor his conjecture, nor doubt."
"15013","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I was told that Abdullah ibn Abi Mulayka says: Umar ibn al-Khattab relieved himself at Ajyad, found a drunken man, brought him to Ibn Abi Mulayka, whom he had made to carry out the hadd punishments, and said: when morning comes, flog him."
"15014","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: a man testified before Shurayh, so Shurayh said: stand, we have known you."
"15015","From al-Thawri, he said: the ruler rejects witnesses by his knowledge; and Shurayh said to a man who testified in something: stand, we have known you."
"15016","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Imran saying: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: allow the testimony of a man for his brother when he is just; Ata said that while I was listening."
"15017","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Muzahim informed me that Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid informed him that Ibn al-Zubayr allowed his testimony for Abdullah ibn Abi Yazid, his brother, and the testimony of Abdullah ibn Abi Yazid for him."
"15018","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: the testimony of two brothers for their brother is valid when they are just."
"15019","Mamar reported from Qatada, he said: the testimony of a brother for his brother is valid when another man is with him."
"15020","Abdullah reported from Shuba, from Uthman al-Batti, he said: I heard al-Shabi saying: the closest valid testimony of relatives is the testimony of the brother."
"15021","Ibn Abi Sabra reported from Abu al-Zinad, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabia, he said: Umar said: the testimony of the father for his son, the son for his father, and the brother for his brother is valid when they are just; Allah did not say, when He said: from those you approve among the witnesses, except that he be a father, a son, or a brother; he said: Amr ibn Sulaym informed me from Ibn al-Musayyib the same, except he did not mention Umar in it."
"15022","Ibn Uyayna reported from Shabib ibn Gharqada, he said: I heard Shurayh allow for a woman the testimony of her father and her husband; a man said to him: he is her father and her husband; Shurayh said to him: who testifies for a woman except her father and her husband."
"15023","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, he said: the testimony of the son for his father is not valid, nor of the father for his son, nor is the testimony of the wife for her husband valid, nor of the husband for his wife."
"15024","Mamar reported from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz allowed the testimony of the son for his father when he is just."
"15025","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: four whose testimony is not valid: the father for his son, the son for his father, the wife for her husband, the husband for his wife, the slave for his master, the master for his slave, and the partner for his partner concerning the thing shared between them; as for anything besides that, his testimony is valid; one who heard Mutarrif informed me, narrating from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, the same, except he did not mention the partner."
"15026","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri and Hammad, they said: the testimony of a contractually-freed slave is not valid."
"15027","Mamar reported from Qatada and Hammad, they said: when part of him is freed and he is working to earn his freedom, his testimony is valid; he said: Hammad said: Ibrahim said: when he is working to earn, he is in the position of the slave, meaning: his testimony is not valid."
"15028","Al-Thawri reported from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: the testimony of the contractually-freed slave is not valid."
"15029","Israel reported from a man he named, from Amir, he said: when half of the slave is freed, his testimony is valid."
"15030","Mamar reported from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Samura, that Umar said: when the contractually-freed slave pays half, there is no slavery upon him."
"15031","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they said: the contractually-freed slaves divorce, wound, testimony, inheritance, and blood-money are in the position of the slave."
"15032","I asked al-Thawri about a man upon whom part of his earning remains, then he testifies; he said: his testimony is valid."
"15033","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they said: when a Christian has testimony, or a slave, or a boy, then he stands with it after the Christian embraces Islam, or the slave is freed, or the boy reaches puberty, their testimony is valid; but if he stood with it before that and it was rejected, it is not valid after that."
"15034","Al-Thawri reported concerning a slave who testifies while a slave, and it is rejected before the judge, then he is freed and testifies with it; he said: Abu Bistam said, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim: his testimony is not valid; and al-Hakam said: it is valid, and it is more beloved to Sufyan; and likewise the boy and the Christian."
"15035","Mamar reported from Qatada, he said: their testimony is valid except in a hadd case, when the Christian embraces Islam, or the slave is freed, or the boy reaches puberty."
"15036","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: a slave and a Christian testify, then this one embraces Islam and this one is freed; he gave me no reply, and said: if you find of Quraysh one who knew knowledge in the pre-Islamic age, then testified with it in Islam, and his testimony was valid, then this is like it."
"15037","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said: the sons of Abi Utba disputed to Sad concerning a quarter-share among them, so Muawiya judged between them by the testimony of al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaa, and that testimony of his was in the pre-Islamic age, and I consider that of it only valid; Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Abi Mulayka informed me of this report of Amr to me, except he added along with al-Muttalib Yala ibn Umayya, so Muawiya allowed their testimony in Islam, while that knowledge of theirs was in the pre-Islamic age."
"15038","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abu Bakr informed me, and I heard it from Abu Bakr, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he said: the testimony of the disbeliever, the boy, and the slave is valid when they did not stand with it in that state of theirs, but testify with it after the disbeliever embraces Islam, the boy grows up, and the slave is freed, when at the time they testify with it they are just; Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Abi Sabra said: Abu al-Nadr informed me, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu al-Zinad, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabia, the same; and Amr claimed their companions are upon it; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, from Ibn Shihab, that that is Sunna; Ibn Jurayj said: I heard Abdullah ibn Abi Mulayka saying similar to that, not attributing it from anyone."
"15039","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Mulayka informed me that he sent to Ibn Abbas, while he was judge for Ibn al-Zubayr, asking him about the testimony of boys; he said: I do not see their testimony valid; Allah commanded us only from those we approve, and a boy is not approved; and Ibn al-Zubayr said to me: likely if they are taken at that moment, if they understand what they saw they are believed, and if another conveys their testimony; he said: and I saw judgment in that only valid upon what Ibn al-Zubayr said."
"15040","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, that he was a judge for Ibn al-Zubayr, so he sent to Ibn Abbas asking him about the testimony of boys, and he did not allow them nor consider their testimony anything; then he asked Ibn al-Zubayr, and he said: when they are brought at the calamity, their testimony is valid; Mamar said: and I heard one saying: their testimony is written, then affirmed until the boy grows, then he is stood upon it; if he knows it, it is valid."
"15041","Al-Thawri reported from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Sulayman al-Hamdani, he said: I testified before Shurayh while a boy, so he poked with his forefinger in my body thus, until you reach puberty, then I will ask you."
"15042","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Ishaq that Shurayh allowed the testimony of boys concerning a slave-girl, in which he judged four thousand."
"15043","Israel reported from Isa ibn Abi Azza, from Amir: he used to allow the testimony of boys, some against others, and call them each year and ask them about it."
"15044","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ismail ibn Muhammad, Yaqub ibn Utba, and Salih claimed: one who has not reached puberty has no testimony."
"15045","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Muhammad ibn Murra informed me, a narration he raised to Ibrahim, that Shurayh allowed the testimony of boys against boys, when they have not dispersed, and affirm upon that when they grow or reach puberty."
"15046","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I was told that Shurayh allowed the testimony of boys; and that Muawiya said: when they are taken at that moment."
"15047","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa informed me, from Urwa, he said: the testimony of boys is valid among themselves, and their first saying is taken."
"15048","Al-Aslami reported from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali, that he said: the first testimony of boys is taken, meaning among themselves."
"15049","He said: Amr informed me, from al-Hasan, from Ali: he used to allow the testimony of boys, some against others, and did not allow their testimony against others of men; he said: Ali did not judge by their testimony except when they say in that state, before their families teach them."
"15050","Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah informed me, from Abu al-Zinad, Amr ibn Sulaym, and Abdullah ibn Muhammad, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: the testimony of boys is valid when they have not dispersed, until one says: they knew, so they learned."
"15051","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: he narrated to me also from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: the Sunna is that the testimony of boys is valid before they disperse."
"15052","Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Shihab was asked about boys playing who broke a boys hand; two testified that a boy among them broke his hand, and two others among them testified against another boy among them that he broke it; he said: the testimony of boys in past time was not accepted, until the first of the rulers who judged with it was Marwan; so when the testimony of boys agrees upon one matter, it is upon what they testified to; but when they differ, we hold their difference rejects their testimony, and we hold that turns to the oaths of whichever of the disputants has reached puberty."
"15053","Mamar reported from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: when a man testifies with two testimonies, the first is accepted and the other left, and he is brought down to the position of a boy; al-Aslami reported from Abu Jabir, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the same."
"15054","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ibn Abi Dhib that he asked Abu Jabir al-Bayadi about a man testifying with a testimony then testifying with another; he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: take by his first saying; he said: they differed upon me in it, among them one who says: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to say: taken by his first saying, and among them one who says: he said: taken by his last saying."
"15055","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, concerning a man asked, it is said: do you have testimony; he says: No, then he testifies after that, that he used to allow his testimony; Sufyan said: our saying is: the witness is given latitude, he increases in his testimony and decreases, as long as the judgment has not passed; but when the judgment has passed and the witness retracts, he guarantees what he testified with."
"15056","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, that Shurayh: a man testified before him with a testimony and the judgment was executed in it, then the man retracted after, and he did not believe his saying."
"15057","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who made a man a witness upon his testimony, then the judge judged by his testimony, then the witness who testified upon his testimony came and said: I did not testify anything; he says: when the judge has judged, the judgment stands."
"15058","Hushaym reported, he said: Yazid ibn Zadawayh informed me that he heard al-Shabi asked about a man against whom two men testify that he divorced his wife, so separated between them by their testimony, then one of the two witnesses marries her after her waiting period ends, then the other witness retracts; al-Shabi said: his retraction is not heeded when the judgment has passed."
"15059","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning two men who testified against a man and judgment was passed against him, then they denied after that and said: our testimonies are false; he said: if they were just on the day they testified, their testimony was valid, and the wealth is returned to the first."
"15060","Mamar reported from Ibn Shubruma: concerning two men who testified against a man with a right, and it was taken from him, then they said: we testified against him falsely, they guarantee it in their wealth."
"15061","From al-Thawri, from Abu Muawiya, he said: I asked al-Shabi, I said: a man makes me a witness against a man with a testimony, then I am brought a writing resembling my writing, and a seal resembling my seal, and I do not remember; al-Shabi said: do not testify until you remember."
"15062","Mamar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father: he used to allow testimony upon recognition of handwriting."
"15063","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Shihab said: in the first time judgment was passed by the testimony of the dead; but when people took wrongs, and writing down the testimony of the dead, judges in the latter time nullified the testimony of the dead, and the claim against every dead person, unless the claimant brings witnesses to the testimony of the dead, or a deed of right until he knows the writers writing; so whoever brings testimony is given by his testimony, and whoever brings a deed knowing its owners handwriting, there are oaths upon the one claimed against, by Allah the claimant of this deed has no right against our companion; but if he refuses to swear, the claimant deserves by his oath, by Allah this deed is true; this is what reached us was judged with in the testimony of the dead in the first and latter time, and Allah knows best about that."
"15064","Mamar reported from Harun ibn Riab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said concerning a man who comes along with the disputant and he thinks he has testimony, while he has no testimony; he said: he is a false witness."
"15065","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir and Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, he said: hearsay testimony is valid; who conceals it conceals testimony."
"15066","Israel reported, he said: Isa ibn Abi Azza informed me, he saw Amir reject the testimony of one hiding, concealed, for a man."
"15067","Ibn Uyayna reported from al-Aswad ibn Qays, he said: I heard Shurayh saying: I do not allow the testimony of one hiding."
"15068","A man reported from al-Shaybani, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Amr ibn Hurayth, he said: the testimony of one hiding is valid; that is done only with the treacherous wicked one."
"15069","Umar ibn Rashid reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: no religious community inherits from another, and the testimony of a community against another is not valid except the nation of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for their testimony is valid against others."
"15070","Zama ibn Salih reported from Ziyad al-Khurasani, from Ibn Shihab, he said: the testimony of Jews against Christians is not valid, nor of Christians against Jews, because of the enmity Allah mentioned between them; he said: And We cast among them enmity and hatred till the Day of Resurrection."
"15071","Mamar reported, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the testimony of the People of the Book, some against others, and he said: it is valid."
"15072","Mamar reported from Qatada and Rabia ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, they said: the testimony of Jews against Christians is not valid, nor is the testimony of Christians against Jews valid; Abd al-Razzaq said: I do not think the interpretation of Mamars narration from al-Zuhri except upon this."
"15073","Al-Thawri said: Abu Husayn informed us, from al-Shabi, he said: the testimony of people of a religion against another religion is not valid except the Muslims."
"15074","Abdullah reported from Shuba, he said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad about the testimony of the Jew against the Christian, and the Christian against the Jew; al-Hakam said: the testimony of people of a religion against another religion is not valid; and Hammad said: their testimony, some against others, is valid when they are just in their religion."
"15075","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Husayn, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from Shurayh: he used to allow the testimony of the People of the Book, some against others."
"15076","Al-Thawri reported from Isa, from al-Shabi, that he used to allow the testimony of the Jew against the Christian, and the Christian against the Jew; and Abu Husayn narrated the opposite of it."
"15077","Al-Thawri reported from Amr ibn Maymun, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: he allowed the testimony of a Magian against a Christian, or a Christian against a Magian."
"15078","Al-Thawri reported concerning a Christian who died, and a Muslim man came with two Christian witnesses that he owes him a thousand dirhams, and a Christian man came with witnesses of Christians that he owes him a thousand dirhams; he said: it is for the Muslim, because the testimony of the Christians harms the right of the Muslim."
"15079","Sufyan said concerning a Christian who bought an animal from a Muslim, then a Christian came claiming it is his animal, bringing witnesses of Christians; he said: he judges against the Christian, and nothing is taken from the Muslim except with proof of Muslims."
"15080","Al-Thawri reported concerning two men whose father died, and one of them said: he died a Christian, and the other said: rather he was a Christian then embraced Islam; and the Muslim brought witnesses of Christians that he had embraced Islam, and the Christian brought witnesses of Muslims that he had not embraced Islam; he said: the testimony of the Christians is valid for his Islam, and the testimony of those who said: he did not embrace Islam, is not valid; and likewise all witnesses who came saying: it was not so, while the others said: it was so, for the testimony of those who said: it was so, is valid."
"15081","Sufyan said about a man who died and left wealth, and a Christian came and said: He is my father, he died a Christian, and a Muslim came and said: He is my father, he died a Muslim. He said: They are only claiming the wealth, so the wealth is between them in halves, but the prayer over him and burial is with the Muslims, when no proof is established."
"15082","Al-Thawri reported from al-Amash from Ibrahim from Shurayh, he said: The testimony of the Jew and the Christian is not allowed except in travel, and in travel it is not allowed except in a will."
"15083","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Zakariya from al-Shabi: That a man from Khatham died in a land of the Sawad, and he called two men from the People of the Book as witnesses over his will, either two Jews or two Christians, and that was raised to Abu Musa al-Ashari, so he made them swear after the Asr prayer by Allah, besides whom there is no god, that it is his will itself, they neither substituted nor changed nor concealed, then he allowed it."
"15084","Mamar reported from Qatadah from Ibn al-Musayyib about His saying: Or two others from among others, he said: From the People of the Book."
"15085","Mamar reported from Ayyub from Ibn Sirin from Ubaydah, he said: Or two others: From the people of the religious community."
"15086","Hisham reported from Mamar from Muhammad from Ubaydah, he said: From the people of the religious community? Al-Thawri said: Disbelief is a community and Islam is a community."
"15087","Mamar reported from Ayyub from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: Kab ibn Sawr used to make the People of the Book swear, placing the Gospel upon his head, then bringing him to the altar and making him swear by Allah."
"15088","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir from al-Shabi from Masruq, he said: He used to make them swear by Allah, and he used to say: Allah revealed: And judge between them by what Allah revealed."
"15089","Israel said: Simak narrated to us from al-Shabi that Abu Musa al-Ashari made a Jew swear by Allah, and Amir said: If only he had made him enter the church."
"15090","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Imran ibn Musa informed me that he attended Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm; they allowed the testimony of the slanderer after he was flogged and had repented."
"15091","Mamar reported from Qatadah from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When the slanderer repents, his testimony is allowed."
"15092","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When the slanderer repents, his testimony is accepted. Al-Zuhri said: And his repentance is that he bely himself."
"15093","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: Three testified against al-Mughirah of adultery, among them Ziyad and Abu Bakrah, and Ziyad withheld, so Umar flogged them and asked them to repent. Two of them repented and Abu Bakrah did not repent, so his testimony was not accepted. He said: And Abu Bakrah is the brother of Ziyad by his mother, and when the matter of Ziyad happened, Abu Bakrah swore not to speak to Ziyad, and he did not speak to him until he died."
"15094","Muhammad ibn Muslim reported: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Four testified against al-Mughirah of adultery, and Ziyad was made to withhold, so Umar flogged the three, then asked them to repent. Two repented and their testimony was accepted, and Abu Bakrah refused to repent, so his testimony was not allowed, and he had become like an arrowhead from worship until he died."
"15095","Al-Thawri reported from Abu al-Haytham, he said: Al-Shabi said to Ibrahim: Why do you not accept the testimony of the slanderer? He said: Because we do not know whether he repented or not."
"15096","From al-Thawri from Ismail, he said: I heard al-Shabi saying: Allah accepts his repentance and you do not accept his testimony, meaning the slanderer."
"15097","Al-Thawri reported from Ashath from al-Shabi from Shurayh, he said: The testimony of every person under a prescribed penalty is allowed except the slanderer; his repentance is between him and his Lord."
"15098","Mamar reported from Qatadah or another from al-Hasan, he said: The testimony of the slanderer is never accepted; his repentance is between him and Allah. Sufyan said: And we hold to that."
"15099","Ibrahim ibn Umar reported from Abd al-Karim from al-Hasan about a slave who was flogged then freed, he said: His testimony is not allowed."
"15100","Al-Thawri said: When the Jew and the Christian are flogged for slander then embrace Islam, their testimony is allowed, because Islam demolishes what was before it, and when the slave is flogged for slander then freed, his testimony is not allowed."
"15101","Malik reported from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr from Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Uthman from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Shall I not inform you of the best of witnesses? The one who renders his testimony before he is asked about it."
"15102","Muhammad ibn Muslim reported from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The best of witnesses is he who renders his testimony before he is asked about it."
"15103","Muhammad ibn Muslim reported from Amr ibn Dinar from Ibn Abbas, he said: If someone has a testimony with you, and he asks you about it, inform him of it and do not say: I will not inform you of it, perhaps he will return or desist."
"15104","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata and Mujahid about His saying: And let no scribe nor witness refuse, they said: It is obligatory upon the scribe to write, and the witnesses are not to refuse, they said: When they had witnessed before that."
"15105","Hushaym reported from Mughirah, he said: I said to Ibrahim: I am invited to a testimony and I fear I will forget. He said: If you wish, do not testify."
"15106","Mamar reported: Abu Hayy informed me that a man asked al-Hasan, saying: O Abu Said, I am invited to testimony while reluctant. He said: If you wish, testify, and if you wish, do not testify."
"15107","Mamar reported from Ibn Tawus from his father about His saying: And let no scribe nor witness be harmed, he said: When he is invited, and he says: I have a need."
"15108","Mamar said, and Qatadah said: And let no scribe be harmed by writing what was not dictated to him, nor a witness by testifying to what he was not called to witness."
"15109","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, he said: And let no scribe nor witness be harmed, that they render what is with them."
"15110","Ibn Jurayj reported: I was informed that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, bought a horse from a Bedouin, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I bought it for such-and-such. The Bedouin said: Rather for such-and-such. Khuzaymah ibn Thabit al-Ansari found them disputing about the price, so Khuzaymah testified for the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Were you present with us? He said: Rather I knew that you are truthful, you say nothing but truth, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made his testimony the testimony of two men."
"15111","Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Umarah informed me from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit that a Bedouin sold a mare to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then he left and added against the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then denied that he had sold it. Khuzaymah ibn Thabit passed by them and heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: I have bought it from you. So he testified to that, and when the Bedouin left, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Were you present with us? He said: No, but when I heard you saying: He has sold to you, I knew it is true, you say nothing but truth. He said: Then your testimony is the testimony of two men."
"15112","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri or Qatadah or both that a Jew came demanding payment from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I have paid you. The Jew said: Your proof. Then Khuzaymah al-Ansari came and said: I testify that he has paid you. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: How do you know? He said: I believe you in greater than that, I believe you in the tidings of heaven. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allowed his testimony as the testimony of two men."
"15113","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri from Kharijah ibn Zayd that Zayd ibn Thabit said: When we wrote the copies of the Quran I missed a verse I used to hear from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and I found it with Khuzaymah ibn Thabit: Among the believers are men true to what they promised Allah, up to a great alteration. He said: And Khuzaymah was called the Possessor of the Two Testimonies; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allowed his testimony as two testimonies. Al-Zuhri said: And he was killed on the day of Siffin."
"15114","My father reported that he asked Wahb, he said: A man who used to pasture horses for Yusuf ibn Umar made us witness over a horse and it died, and when he came seeking testimony from us, our companion said: It is male, and the one who made us witness said: Rather it is female. If we testify that it is male, he will kill him with whips. Wahb said: Testify with what he told you, and save him from this tyrant."
"15115","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is His saying: Then contract them if you know good in them? He said: We see it only as wealth. Then he recited: Prescribed for you when death attends one of you, if he leaves good, the will. He said: Good is wealth, as we see, in ingots. He said: I said to him: What if I do not know wealth with him and he is a truthful man? He said: I do not reckon good except as wealth. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I reckon all that as wealth, and righteousness."
"15116","Ibn Jurayj said: And it reached me from Ibn Abbas, he said: If you know good in them, good is wealth, and Mujahid said it. He said: Good is wealth, whatever their manners and religion may be. From al-Thawri from Layth from Mujahid, he said: It is wealth."
"15117","From Mamar from Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Ubaydah al-Salmani about His saying: Then contract them if you know good in them, he said: If you know trustworthiness with them."
"15118","From Hisham ibn Hassan from Muhammad from Ubaydah, he said: If they establish prayer."
"15119","From al-Thawri from Yunus ibn Ubayd from al-Hasan, he said: Religion and trustworthiness."
"15120","From al-Thawri from Mughirah from Ibrahim, he said: If you know good in them, he said: Truthfulness and fidelity."
"15121","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: Is it obligatory upon me when I know wealth for him to contract him? He said: I see it only as obligatory. And Amr ibn Dinar said it. I said to Ata: Do you narrate it from anyone? He said: No."
"15122","From Mamar from Qatadah, he said: Sirin, the father of Muhammad, asked Anas ibn Malik for manumission by contract, and Anas refused, so Umar ibn al-Khattab raised the stick over him and recited: Then contract them, so Anas contracted him."
"15123","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: An informant informed me that Musa ibn Anas ibn Malik informed him that Sirin asked Anas ibn Malik for the contract, and he had much wealth, but he refused, so he went to Umar ibn al-Khattab and sought enforcement against him. Umar said to Anas: Contract him, but he refused, so he struck him with the stick and said: Contract him. Anas said: I will not contract him, so he struck him with the stick and recited: Then contract them if you know good in them, so Anas contracted him."
"15124","From al-Thawri from al-Shabi, he said: If he wishes he contracts his slave, and if he wishes he does not contract him."
"15125","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I think he will give me nothing except from begging people, is there sin upon me not to contract him? He said: I do not like that, and I see nothing upon you if you do not contract him."
